\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong money market rates jump as c.bank soaks up cash\b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2017\par 05:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nov 3 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's money market rates spiked this week to multi-year highs, reacting afresh to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts in the past few months to soak up excess cash, push rates closer to U.S. yields and put a floor under its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Three-month interbank rates have climbed 22 basis points in three weeks to 0.985 percent, their highest since early February, which is a rare move in a market whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is loosely pegged to the U.S. dollar and whose interest rates shadow U.S. yields.\par \par \uc2 Market participants said cash supplies had tightened after a spurt in initial public offerings of shares by companies in the past couple of months along with a sudden increase in issuance of bills by the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA).\par \par \uc2 It appeared the HKMA was trying to soak up the excess cash in the banking system so that domestic rates would re-align more closely with rising U.S. yields, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "It is probably a precautionary measure as low HIBORs may lead to complacency in the markets and also stoke speculation in the Hong Kong dollar," said Eugene Leow, a strategist with \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "The bill issuances are part of their open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . More issuances will help tighten liquidity and lift HIBORs closer to LIBORs."\par \par \uc2 HIBOR-LIBOR spreads, the differential between floating rates at which banks lend to each other in Hong Kong and the U.S. respectively, had been gradually widening since the beginning of the year as markets priced in a steady rise in U.S. interest rates. The spread was 2 basis points at the beginning of the year, moved to as wide as 81 basis points at the end of August and is now half that level.\par \par \uc2 The HKMA issued HK$80 billion ($10.3 billion) worth of Exchange Fund Bills (EFBs) since early August, helping push up HIBOR. On both occasions when it announced the issuance of the bills, the HKMA said the issuance had nothing to do with the Hong Kong dollar's weakening since the beginning of this year.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar, but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85. The HKMA is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hits either end of the band to keep it intact.\par \par \uc2 Flush liquidity in the economy had driven the Hong Kong dollar down by nearly 1 percent between January and early September, close to the weak end of its band. It has since recovered to around 7.8 per U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Alongside the rise in HIBOR, the sum of balances maintained by commercial banks with the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the 'aggregate balance', has fallen from about HK$259.57 billion in the last week of August to HK$179.73 billion by the end of October.\par \par \uc2 "The issuance of additional EFBs and tightened liquidity is providing support to the Honky," said Gao Qi, an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotiabank in Singapore, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by its nickname.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had depreciated a lot since the beginning of the year, so it is a correction and could be attributed to tightened Hong Kong dollar liquidity conditions."\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to have stabilised in the middle of the band. They had expected the HKMA to issue more bills last week, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not do so.\par \par \uc2 Yet, they also expect the HKMA to keep a close eye on HIBOR and ensure it doesn't lag U.S. rates.\par \par \uc2 "Low rates may lead to asset bubbles being built as investors get complacent," said Leow of \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2 . "This could prove problematic in an environment where the G10 economies are tightening policy."\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 7.8015 Hong Kong dollars) (Reporting by Rushil Dutta in Bengaluru; Editing by Vidya Ranganathan and Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-11-3T05:15:02.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171103edb3006bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
