\par\par \b \uc2 Why Hong Kong's Rally May Be Cut Short -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Shane \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2017\par 05:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, boring is good. The city prides itself on efficiency and its ability to put business first. That applies to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , too, which has been pegged to the U.S. dollar for three decades.\par \par \uc2 But the boring Hong Kong dollar could soon get investors' pulses racing. The local Hang Seng Index is among the world's top performing markets year to date, returning more than 22%. However, the weakening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens to derail that rally.\par \par \uc2 That isn't something investors normally worry about. The Hong Kong dollar trades in a narrow band versus the greenback. If it approaches either end of that range, the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or HKMA -- the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- steps in to buy or sell Hong Kong dollars and steady the ship. To maintain that equilibrium, the authority follows U.S. monetary policy. The system has its detractors, but it has served the financial hub reasonably well.\par \par \uc2 However, the Hong Kong dollar hasn't been following the normal script this year. From early January to mid-August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid from the lower end of its band (meaning it was stronger relative to the dollar) to the precipice of the higher end (weaker).\par \par \uc2 IT'S EASY TO SEE WHY: The authority's base rate followed the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's recent interest-rate hikes, but Hong Kong's banks didn't. The three-month U.S. dollar-based London interbank borrowing rate is now about 70% higher than its Hong Kong dollar-based equivalent. The prime rate, the benchmark for mortgage rates at Hong Kong banks, hasn't budged since the financial crisis. That's because the city's banks are flush with cash from years of quantitative easing and inflows from the mainland. This cheap money has fueled the bull market in Hong Kong stocks and real estate.\par \par \uc2 That could soon change. The closer the Hong Kong dollar creeps to the high end of its band, the more likely it is that the HKMA will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and mopping up liquidity. Fewer \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars in the system mean banks will start charging more to lend them. "That will have some impact on equity investors because the cost of funds will increase," Alex Wong, director of asset management at Ample Capital, tells Barron's. In mid-August, the HKMA sold some debt to soak up liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Interest-rate-sensitive stocks would be among the obvious casualties. Think Hong Kong real estate, for example. Higher mortgage rates could cool red-hot demand for property -- that wouldn't bode well for developers such as \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (ticker: 16.Hong Kong) or \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (83.Hong Kong).\par \par \uc2 There's an added complication to the Hang Seng's bull run. Three blue-chip stocks -- \uc2 Tencent Holdings\uc2  (700.Hong Kong), \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (5.Hong Kong), and insurer AIA Group (1299.Hong Kong) -- have all been strong and account for 20% of the market valuation. "That's why the Hang Seng Index isn't weak" despite the sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Wong. Fears about rates could stoke market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong investors who are braced for higher rates should focus on undervalued names that won't feel ill effects when the era of cheap Hong Kong dollars ends. Ample's Wong says Macau gaming stocks look like a good bet right now because they will benefit long-term from plans to link the enclave to Hong Kong and mainland China via an ambitious new bridge network.\par \par \uc2 Email: daniel.shane@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 Like Barron's on \uc2 Facebook\par \par \uc2 Follow Barron's on \uc2 Twitter\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2017 23:40 ET (03:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170812ed8c0001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
