\par\par \b \uc2 No Need to Scrap Hong Kong's US Dollar Peg,  \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority \highlight\uc2  Chief Says -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Routh \par \uc2 252 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2017\par 04:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 There is no need for Hong Kong to do away with its three-decade old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar, according to the head of its defacto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The head of the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) \uc2 Norman Chan\uc2  was cited by Bloomberg as saying:\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong is a small and open economy. Keeping a stable exchange rate between the Hong Kong dollar and the U.S. dollar is the most suitable arrangement. We have no need and no intention to change such an effective system.\par \par \uc2 The city's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pegged to the greenback since 1983. The Hong Kong dollar is permitted to trade in a tight band (HKD7.75 to HKD7.85 per U.S. dollar). If it exceeds this band at either end, the HKMA will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There has often been speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  will switch its peg to the Chinese renminbi. Chan listed four considerations before this could happen: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- A fully convertible yuan \par  \par  \par    -- Open capital account with no capital controls \par  \par  \par    -- A financial market with sufficient depth and width that allows Hong \par       Kong's Exchange Fund to hold assets to support the city's monetary base \par  \par  \par    -- Synchronized economic cycles between Hong Kong and China \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/asia-stocks-to-watch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/asia-stocks-to-watch}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 18, 2017 22:18 ET (02:18 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170619ed6j0004c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
