\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Scores Biggest Jump in 5 Months -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan and Shen Hong \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2017\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B11\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the yuan to its biggest one-day jump in roughly five months Thursday, the latest sign that authorities are seeking to bolster the yuan in the wake of the downgrade of the country's sovereign debt by \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 The yuan fix was 0.8% stronger than Wednesday's, as China set the dollar's daily midpoint for trading at 6.8090 yuan, compared with 6.8633 yuan. It was the yuan's highest level since Nov. 10. The jump wasn't entirely surprising, traders said: The yuan ended onshore trading Wednesday at 6.8210 to the dollar, its strongest point since Nov. 11, after a 0.6% surge that day.\par \par \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's model had predicted the fix would set the dollar at 6.8136 yuan, while a Shanghai-based senior trader with a domestic bank said his own model expected a fix of about 6.8100.\par \par \uc2 In setting the daily fix, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  considers where the yuan ended against the dollar in the previous day's onshore trading, the overnight movement of a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a "countercyclical" factor that was unveiled last week. Within China, the yuan trades in a tight band around that fix. In theory, it trades freely offshore, in major hubs like \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the onshore market in recent days, traders say, directing state-owned banks to buy yuan and sell dollars. Without that, the yuan wouldn't have become so strong, said the Shanghai-based trader.\par \par \uc2 "The key question for everyone now is when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will let this round of appreciation end," the trader said. "These are quite uncertain times for yuan traders."\par \par \uc2 As to why the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is propping up the yuan, analysts say there are several theories. It could be meant to deter investors from betting the yuan will decline following the \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade. Or it could be to help make China's domestic bonds more attractive to foreign investors: The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has approved a bond-connect program between the mainland and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore market, the yuan advanced 0.2% to 6.7309 against the dollar in the offshore market on Thursday, following a sharp rise the day before. Market participants say the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has likely been behind a surge in short-term borrowing costs for the yuan in Hong Kong, which has pushed the yuan sharply higher in recent days.\par \par \uc2 The cost for banks to borrow yuan from each other overnight in Hong Kong rose to 42.82% on Thursday from 21.08% on Wednesday. The Chinese authorities have pushed up these rates in the past 18 months to squeeze investors out of bearish bets on the yuan, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 While investors in the options markets are now paying up for contracts that protect against a rise in the offshore yuan in the next month, they still expect a decline over the longer run.\par \par \uc2 "With the cyclical peak in growth in the rear mirror, it is just a matter of time until the authorities need to get back to facilitating depreciation in the trade weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Jason Daw, head of emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 , wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com and Shen Hong at hong.shen@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected on June 1, 2017 at 2359 GMT to reflect that \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  may be propping up the yuan to deter investors from betting against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. The original version incorrectly said the intent may be to deter bets against the yen, when it should've been yuan.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 01, 2017 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170601ed61000kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
