\par\par \b \uc2 Wall Street Struggles to Cash In on Yuan's Swoon\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan and Carolyn Cui \par \uc2 971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2017\par 03:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Across Wall Street, many see further declines in the Chinese yuan as a nearly sure thing. But making money on them is proving anything but.\par \par \uc2 Investors and analysts widely expect that the yuan will fall against the dollar this year, extending a 6.6% drop in 2016 that was the largest in two decades. The bearishness reflects China's slowing economy, rising debt and volatile capital outflows, as well as concern that the world's most-populous nation could be headed for a trade showdown with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 China reported Friday that its economy grew by 6.8% in the fourth quarter and 6.7% for 2016. While it met China's target, the annual rate is the lowest in a quarter of a century.\par \par \uc2 But bets on yuan declines generally have performed poorly in recent years, reflecting factors including high borrowing rates in China and Hong Kong. In addition, aggressive efforts this year by Chinese authorities to punish short sellers are discouraging some yuan bets.\par \par \uc2 Valentina Chen, a Zurich-based emerging-market bond manager at Vontobel Asset Management, said she is "very comfortable" that the yuan will depreciate against the dollar over time, but "in the short term it is very difficult to trade."\par \par \uc2 In part, that is because so many hedge funds and other asset managers have been shorting the yuan, pushing up trading costs.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors typically use forward contracts or put options in the offshore market, centered in Hong Kong. Holding a 12-month forward contract costs 4.8% annually, after reaching as high as 6.6% in early January, up from 2.5% last year. Holding an option to sell yuan is also expensive, recently costing 5.1% annually.\par \par \uc2 Denver-based Crescat Capital has been betting against the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years but trimmed its short position late last year by letting two of its three put options expire, said Chief Investment Officer Kevin Smith. Instead, the company has begun to focus on bets against Chinese banks on the idea that a credit crisis is likely to emerge before a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis.\par \par \uc2 Colin Harte, a multiasset portfolio manager at BNP Paribas Investment Partners in London, said he is betting on a decline in the new Taiwan dollar instead, since it is cheaper. Desmond Soon, head of investment management for Asia, ex-Japan, at Western Asset Management in Singapore, has been holding fewer Chinese bonds rather than shorting yuan.\par \par \uc2 Shorting the yuan is "not as easy as you would think," said Mr. Smith of Crescat Capital. The firm's bearish bets against China returned 0.2% in 2016.\par \par \uc2 This week, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  didn't roll over its expiring medium-term lending facility to banks as many had expected, adding to concerns about a liquidity squeeze and sending the one-day funding cost to as high as 2.7%. Some short sellers in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were forced out due to higher funding costs.\par \par \uc2 Some investors are also constrained by factors that increase the effectiveness of official \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which authorities drive up borrowing costs in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to force those betting against the yuan with borrowed money to close out their trades at a loss.\par \par \uc2 Many hedge funds and other traders trade on margin, using borrowed money that can become more expensive in a short squeeze, or must report their results periodically to investors, raising the prospect of unhappy questions when results are negative.\par \par \uc2 China first allowed the yuan to trade outside its borders in 2010, creating a parallel exchange rate known as CNH. Average daily spot volume in the CNH market is about $10 billion to $12 billion, roughly one-third of the $35 billion within mainland China, according to \uc2 HSBC\uc2  estimates.\par \par \uc2 But that market's activity has been declining amid rising borrowing costs, exacerbating some of the swings in short-term yuan funding costs. Yuan deposits in Hong Kong stood at 627.6 billion yuan in November, down 37% from a peak of 1 trillion yuan at the end of 2014, according to the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's quite clear now that the Chinese are very active in the offshore market, if and when they want to be," said Dwyfor Evans, head of Asia-Pacific macro strategy at \uc2 State Street Global Markets\uc2 . "It's not as freely traded as we initially thought," he added.\par \par \uc2 Everyone isn't squeezed equally. Some groups such as affluent investors with long time horizons can maintain the trade if it pays out modest but regular returns over time, said Sharmin Mossavar-Rahmani, chief investment officer of Goldman Sachs Private Wealth Management Group.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mossavar-Rahmani's group has been recommending that clients short the yuan against the dollar since mid-2015, contending China could face a financial crisis spurred by heavy capital outflows and a sharp increase in debt. She said the trade has been profitable.\par \par \uc2 Citing the risk of further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Goldman recommends that clients limit their exposures relative to the size of their portfolios and diversify by holding options to exit from the trade at various levels and times.\par \par \uc2 Axel Merk\uc2 , chief investment officer at Merk Investments LLC in San Francisco, said he has added to his short position on the yuan recently. He holds longer-dated forwards in the offshore market that are less affected by spikes in short-term rates and is waiting for China to focus more on economic reform than on punishing short sellers.\par \par \uc2 "The way you fight capital flight is to make your country more attractive to investors," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com and Carolyn Cui at carolyn.cui@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 23-01-17 0214GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020170123ed1n0002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
