\par\par \b \uc2 China Doubles Down on Defense of Yuan -- 2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan \par \uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2017\par 13:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--China continued to squeeze the global market for the yuan Friday, sending the cost of borrowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in overseas markets soaring to a near-record high.\par \par \uc2 Investors and analysts say the nosebleed rates are likely to continue as \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles to keep the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening too far and fast.\par \par \uc2 The rate that banks charge each other in Hong Kong's overnight lending market for the yuan jumped to 61.3% on Friday--the highest in a year and the second-highest level on record. That rate was 38.3% on Thursday, and has remained above 10% since Dec. 30.\par \par \uc2 Although that offshore yuan-lending rate isn't set by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can effectively control it by directing state lenders to withhold yuan from other banks, something it has been doing recently.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  also set its daily "fix"--the price that determines the yuan's trading range in the domestic market--0.9% stronger against the U.S. dollar on Friday, its biggest increase since 2005 when the bank surprised markets by removing its dollar peg.\par \par \uc2 Despite those moves, the yuan weakened Friday both onshore and off--falling 0.6% to 6.9230 versus the dollar in the domestic market and 1% to 6.8578 in late trading in Hong Kong. That eroded some of the yuan's sharp appreciation from earlier in the week, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped 2.5% against the dollar in two days on the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's steps to tighten liquidity offshore.\par \par \uc2 One of the primary targets of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's moves is investors trying to bet on a decline in the yuan by borrowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Hong Kong, swapping it for dollars and later swapping it back at a more favorable rate. That trade becomes more expensive as yuan borrowing costs rise, which can force investors to abandon those bets by buying back yuan, in turn driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.\par \par \uc2 "It's super painful to hold short positions in [the yuan] when the funding costs are soaring as they are at the moment," says Christoffer Moltke-Leth, director of global sales trading at Saxo Capital Markets in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 However, some investors say Chinese officials could also be anxious about the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which have been falling as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  taps them to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The reserves are currently just above a psychologically important level of $3 trillion, and investors say a fall below that level Saturday--when December's figures are set to be revealed--could spark more trading volatility in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Some traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has an incentive to stem the yuan's decline in the days leading up to the Jan. 20 inauguration of U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who has accused China of keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too cheap.\par \par \uc2 Another milestone is an exchange rate of 7.0 yuan to the dollar, which would be the lowest level since mid-2008, and a potential trigger for more investors to dump the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market watchers say. The yuan approached that level earlier this week, before the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  stepped in to dry up liquidity offshore.\par \par \uc2 "Our impression is that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is very sensitive about the key 7.0 level for the yuan," says Liu Weiming, chief investment officer at Fu Xi Investment Management Pte. Ltd. "You could say that the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mostly about defending that level. Once 7 is broken, people will expect 8 and it will get even worse."\par \par \uc2 Although the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger than 7.0 yuan to the dollar for now, many market watchers say a weaker level is inevitable. Contracts to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a year's time suggest that investors expect an exchange rate of 7.1975 yuan to the dollar at that time. While that rate has come down this week, implying a stronger yuan, it hasn't fallen below 7.0 since mid-November.\par \par \uc2 That means the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will likely continue to tighten offshore liquidity and send investors running out of negative yuan bets in the next few weeks, as well as bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a wave of outflows as residents exchange yuan for foreign cash.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment the bias is to control" the yuan, says Brad Gibson, a fixed-income portfolio manager at AllianceBernstein in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 06, 2017 07:29 ET (12:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170106ed16001cf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
