\par\par \b \uc2 Heard on the Street: Beijing Is Squeezing the Yuan Market -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anjani Trivedi \par \uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2016\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C8\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 A yuan credit crunch isn't what Beijing had in mind when it promoted trading of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  offshore.\par \par \uc2 But that's what it has achieved in Hong Kong of late. For the second time this year, short-term borrowing costs in the yuan have skyrocketed. In recent days, the rate on one-week interbank yuan loans in Hong Kong surged over 8 percentage points to 10.15%. That again makes betting against the yuan an expensive proposition.\par \par \uc2 It's also a reminder that just because the yuan's recent behavior has been seemingly anodyne, markets shouldn't get too complacent.\par \par \uc2 One possible cause of the dysfunction: Beijing's meddling from a year ago. It may feel like a distant memory, but in the weeks after its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated in August 2015, Beijing surreptitiously \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilize the offshore yuan, which had become far weaker than its onshore variant. It did so again nine months ago, at which time a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official acknowledged the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One suspected form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was through positions in longer-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards made by Chinese state banks. Last week, billions of dollars of yuan concentrated at a handful of banks were expected to mature as those one-year contracts came due, traders say. That didn't happen and banks started buying up whatever offshore yuan was available. The problem may have been exacerbated by this week's lunar holiday, in which markets are shut in both Hong Kong and the mainland.\par \par \uc2 The market would've hardly noticed the squeeze if the contracts matured, however the contracts would've had to be acknowledged on either the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's or other conduit banks' balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 This all comes as forwards markets are again pricing in a sharp depreciation in the yuan. While China's reserves have stopped being depleted, capital outflows have continued and even picked up in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Beijing squeezed the offshore yuan a year ago. Investors should be prepared for the effects to keep on lingering.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anjani Trivedi at anjani.trivedi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2016 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160915ec9f000h6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
