\par\par \b \uc2 Surge in Offshore Yuan Borrowing Rate Suggests China  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,086 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2016\par 11:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The most vibrant market for yuan trading outside mainland China has turned into a key battleground for Beijing to defend the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Chinese banks in what's known as \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "offshore" market has led to a surge in the cost for banks in the territory to borrow yuan from each other. Investors and analysts believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14which they say has likely come at the behest of \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14is aimed at thwarting bets against the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the renminbi.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the overnight interbank yuan borrowing rate in Hong Kong reached 5.5155%, its highest since Feb. 19.\par \par \uc2 The rate breached 5% on Thursday, ending a nearly two-month stretch of relative calm, and has since remained at levels that traders characterized as elevated.\par \par \uc2 The suspected heavy buying by Chinese banks has helped squeeze a market China had tried to foster just a few years ago as it looked to promote the yuan as a major international trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It comes on top of other recent measures from Beijing intended to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as tightening restrictions on money flowing out of the mainland.\par \par \uc2 "With costs so high, it's very difficult to conduct renminbi business here," said an executive at one of China's top four state banks operating in Hong Kong. "It's pretty obvious that renminbi internationalization has taken a back seat" to defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the executive said.\par \par \uc2 In response to The Wall Street Journal, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  said "the stated views and analyses are not true." It didn't elaborate. President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  said last week that yuan liberalization will be carried out in an "orderly" way and China will continue to encourage the yuan's use abroad.\par \par \uc2 Yuan-related trading has been falling in Hong Kong since Beijing first put a squeeze on the market earlier this year, according to the latest data. Deposits denominated in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have dropped to 667.1 billion yuan ($99.9 billion) in July from a peak of just over 1 trillion yuan in December 2014, according to the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yuan bonds issuance has dropped to $6.3 billion so far this year from $16.2 billion in the same period a year ago, according to Dealogic.\par \par \uc2 The declining interest in using the yuan for trading and capital-raising has come as Beijing's priorities have shifted, with the Chinese economy losing steam after years of breathtaking growth\u8212\'20\'14and steady yuan appreciation. Beijing first allowed the yuan to be bought and sold outside the mainland in 2010, in Hong Kong, still the world's major center for offshore trading in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, a more urgent task for China is to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling too much and potentially further eroding investors' confidence in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Since early this year, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trying to let some air out of the yuan while trying to make sure that the descent doesn't become so fast as to trigger excessive capital outflows. That strategy often risks getting derailed by investors who use the offshore market in Hong Kong, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be traded freely, to wager against the yuan. (In the mainland, the yuan is only allowed to swing 2% above or below the level set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  each day.)\par \par \uc2 The more bearish bets pile up on the yuan offshore, the more difficult it is for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control its descent. These bets are typically enacted by traders who the borrow the yuan overnight, and exchange it for dollars, exchanging the money back the following day after the yuan has dropped\u8212\'20\'14a practice known as "shorting." A rise in overnight borrowing costs makes this a less profitable trade.\par \par \uc2 Following a surprise weakening of the yuan's official rate by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early January, investors piled on bets against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Hong Kong's market, causing a widening divergence between the yuan traded there and its mainland peer. Chinese authorities at the time responded by dramatically tightening yuan liquidity in Hong Kong, by ordering local branches of Chinese state-owned banks to buy up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sending the overnight yuan-borrowing cost to a record 66.8%.\par \par \uc2 Then, as now, downward pressure on the yuan\u8212\'20\'14and upward pressure on the dollar\u8212\'20\'14was exacerbated by expectations the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might soon raise interest rates in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 This time around, many investors spotted similar tactics from Beijing as bearish yuan wagers in Hong Kong rose ahead of a Group-of-20 economic summit in China last week. Some investors placed the bets in belief that China would have fewer incentives to hold the yuan steady after the high-level gathering, which Beijing had hoped would showcase the country's economic might.\par \par \uc2 "We see consistent interest in shorting [the yuan offshore]," said Ben Sy, head of fixed income, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodities at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase & Co\uc2 .'s private bank in Asia. Mr. Sy said clients had started to inquire about putting on such bets last month when the yuan traded at 6.63 a dollar in Hong Kong. The dollar slipped 0.1% in late Asian trade to 6.6913 yuan in the offshore market. "People are still looking for opportunities to put a bearish trade on," he added.\par \par \uc2 Such negative sentiment has resulted in the squeeze of yuan funds in Hong Kong's market in recent sessions, as many say Beijing is again putting those short-sellers on alert.\par \par \uc2 "It doesn't look normal to me," said Iris Pang, senior economist for Greater China at investment bank \uc2 Natixis\uc2 , part of France's \uc2 Groupe BPCE\uc2 . "I'm more convinced (this is) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Despite growing depreciation pressure, many investors expect the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to prevent rapid weakening of the yuan\u8212\'20\'14already the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia this year\u8212\'20\'14ahead of the yuan's upcoming inclusion in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's official reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, scheduled for Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's overarching goal is to "prevent any big eruptions," said Sean Chang, head of Asian debt investment at \uc2 Baring Asset Management\uc2  (Asia) Ltd.\par \par \uc2 Carol Chan contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com and Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswir