\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian stocks tread water on wary outlook; bonds firm\b0\par\par\uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2016\par 08:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Hong Kong leads regional markets lower\par \par \uc2 * World stocks had best day in a month on Tuesday\par \par \uc2 * Yen buoyed by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threats from Tokyo\par \par \uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , May 11 (Reuters) - Asian shares hovered near a two-month low on Wednesday, as investors shrugged off an overnight rally in global stocks and looked to bonds in the absence of signs of a sustainable recovery in China and other emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 MSCI\uc2 's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was flat after spending most of the morning session in negative territory. On Tuesday, it hit an eight-week low.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting the cautious mood in Asia, European shares were expected to open broadly steady.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares led regional stocks lower with the benchmark index falling 0.8 percent, followed by Korea down 0.03 percent and Taiwan losing 0.3 percent. They are most vulnerable to weakness in the Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 While strong March data out of China had raised hopes that its economy was turning the corner, mixed data so far in April and surging debt levels in a variety of industries has fuelled doubts about the sustaniability of any recovery in growth.\par \par \uc2 A front page article in the official People's Daily this week that said China's economic trend would be "L-shaped" reinforced investors' doubts over the stock market's potential.\par \par \uc2 "You don't expect a bull market in an L-shaped economy," said David Dai, Shanghai-based investor director at Nanhai Fund Management Co.\par \par \uc2 Japanese shares were among the rare bright spots in the region, with the Nikkei up 0.3 percent as the yen moved further away from the 18-month highs against the dollar struck last week. Overall sentiment remained cautious.\par \par \uc2 "I'd think the markets are supported by lack of negative news flows. It's not that we have clear reason to be positive about the global economy but there may be a bit of unwinding in excessively pessimistic bets," said Hirokazu Kabeya, chief global strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 MSCI\uc2 's broad gauge of global stocks climbed nearly 1.1 percent, its best session in about a month. The U.S. S&P 500 jumped 1.3 percent, tallying its best day in two months.\par \par \uc2 Bonds remained well supported, indicating investors were wary about the prospects for riskier assets in the near term in an environment of sluggish global growth.\par \par \uc2 An auction of three-year U.S. notes on Tuesday was received well. Yields on 10-year debt were at 1.75 percent, not far away from a 2016 low of 1.53 percent.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bonds also reflected the cautious undertone in global markets, with the yield 10-year bonds staying stuck in a narrow range around 0.095 percent.\par \par \uc2 In credit markets, a high yield corporate bond Exchange traded fund (ETF) and its investment grade ETF counterpart have risen in recent weeks indicating a growing preference for bonds.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the yen stayed on the defensive, following two sessions of steep declines after Japanese officials stepped up warnings about potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen was trading at 108.76 to the dollar, having slipped 3 percent from its 18-month high of 105.55 set on May 3.\par \par \uc2 The dollar got broad support from comments by a top \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  official last week, which kept alive otherwise diminishing hopes of a Fed interest rate hike following soft U.S. payrolls data last Friday.\par \par \uc2 New York Fed President \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  said that it was reasonable to expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise interest rates twice in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's index against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to a near two-week high of 94.150 on Tuesday and last stood at 94.124, having recovered 2.5 percent from its 16-month low touched on Tuesday last week.\par \par \uc2 The euro traded at $1.13820, retreating further from a 2016 high of $1.16160 tested last week.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were supported by disruptions to crude supplies in Canada, Nigeria and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures dipped to $45.28 per barrel, having jumped 4 percent on Tuesday. U.S. crude futures were at $44.40 per barrel. Both were off about 0.5 percent. (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano in TOKYO and Samuel Shen in SHANGHAI; Editing by Shri Navaratnam and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-11T07:06:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160511ec5b0035x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
