\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Use of yuan for trade deals seen growing despite volatility,  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2016\par 00:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story sent on Tuesday)               \par \par \uc2 By Michelle Chen               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Feb 23 (Reuters) - More China trade is expected to be settled in yuan this year despite concerns about its cooling economy and fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could weaken further.               \par \par \uc2 Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months to steady the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and deter speculators have raised questions about China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and threatened to slow Beijing's steady push to internationalise the "redback".               \par \par \uc2 But analysts say that while many companies may now be more cautious about using the yuan, the number of transactions settled in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to grow.               \par \par \uc2 One key factor is the attractiveness of arbitrage activities that allow firms to benefit from differences in interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in mainland China and in Hong Kong.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan's overseas expansion has slowed since last year, with yuan deposits shrinking and the issuance of "dim sum" yuan bonds falling in Hong Kong as demand from foreigners to hold yuan-denominated assets has waned.               \par \par \uc2 However, yuan cross-border trade settlement still grew in 2015, rising to 26 percent of China's total trade from 22 percent a year earlier, according to Reuters calculations.               \par \par \uc2 Some market players expect that could climb to over 30 percent by the end of this year despite worries of another flare-up in volatility.               \par \par \uc2 "There are two arbitrage opporunities here. One is interest rate arbitrage, which will be lasting for the whole of 2016," said Iris Pang, a senior Greater China economist at \uc2 Natixis\uc2 .               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate differentials may also come into play if the gap between onshore and offshore yuan values widens again, Pang said.               \par \par \uc2 Companies benefit from these differences in various ways, either through genuine trading activity or fake trade invoices.               \par \par \uc2 A company can convert U.S. dollars to yuan offshore where the dollar is stronger, and then transmit the funds to a mainland-related firm via a trade settlement. That firm converts the yuan back to dollars at a better rate, booking a profit.               \par \par \uc2 The spread between onshore yuan and its offshore counterpart surged to 1,400 pips in January before narrowing to around 50 pips on Tuesday.               \par \par \uc2 For companies that make use of different interest rates in the two markets, they can get over 4 percent from Hong Kong banks for depositing yuan for one year and then use the deposits as collateral for letters of credit (LCs).               \par \par \uc2 The LCs can be sent to related companies in the mainland, which are granted loans at a cost of around 3 percent from onshore banks based on those LCs, according to \uc2 Natixis\uc2 .               \par \par \uc2 "I don't see strong momentum of yuan trade settlement growth for now as many companies have been taking a wait-and-see approach on whether to use the yuan due to high volatility," said Ngan Kim Man, deputy head of treasury at \uc2 China Everbright Bank\uc2 's Hong Kong branch.               \par \par \uc2 But he added yuan trade settlement volumes may still rise this year if arbitrage activities or fake invoicing continued.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-24T00:30:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160223ec2n029he\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
