\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong's Dollar Peg: Next in the Firing Line? -- The Numbers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gregor Hunter and Wei Gu   \par \uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2016\par 09:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Price of a Big Mac in Hong Kong\par \par \uc2 $2.48\par \par \uc2 A Big Mac costs US$2.48 in Hong Kong--about half of the $4.95 a Big Mac attack would cost you in the U.S. That's surprising given Hong Kong's reputation as one of the most expensive cities in the world to live. The Big Mac index by The Economist, which measures purchasing power parity, is a lighthearted guide to whether \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are at their "correct" level. By this yardstick, the Hong Kong dollar is undervalued by 50% when compared to the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Price Per Square Meter of Luxury Property\par \par \uc2 $50,000\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong dollars don't give much bang of the buck when it comes to buying homes though. For $1 million dollars, one can buy 20 square meters in Hong Kong, making it the second-most expensive in the world after Monaco, according to Knight Frank, a property agency. Home prices have been rising sharply in the past few years, partially because capital inflows can't be reflected in a stronger exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Trading range of Hong Kong dollar against the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 7.75-7.85\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is permitted to trade in a tight band, and if it exceeds either end, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 --selling or buying foreign assets as required. This month, the Hong Kong dollar hit the weakest level in almost 10 years as investors tried to make bets against China.\par \par \uc2 How much consumer prices went up in December, compared to a year ago\par \par \uc2 2.5%\par \par \uc2 While many developed economies such as the U.S., the U.K and Japan have struggled with deflation, Hong Kong is still seeing inflation in consumer prices beyond levels elsewhere. Housing costs play a big role, accounting for 31.7% of the inflation basket, but inflation has been slipping as the property market stumbles.\par \par \uc2 Total inside Hong Kong's warchest of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves\par \par \uc2 US$358.8 billion\par \par \uc2 The foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held in reserve by the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the city's de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , put it in the top 10 among countries worldwide. Some investors have tried--and failed--to break the peg before, most notably George Soros' \uc2 Quantum Fund\uc2  in 1998. The city has plenty of firepower to repel another effort.\par \par \uc2 Number of places pegging their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 42\par \par \uc2 Dozens of countries maintain an official fixed exchange rate to the U.S. dollar, though the number is dwindling--they represented 22.5% of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's member countries as of 2014, down from 33.0% in 2008. Kazakhstan, Vietnam and Azerbaijan abandoned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrangements linking their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar during 2015.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's loss on its investments in 2015\par \par \uc2 HK$18.3bn ($2.35bn)\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Exchange Fund, which manages the city's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, took heavy losses last year, mostly from its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions. Though its investment return of -0.6% in 2015 was the first since 2008, it doesn't come close to the 5.6% loss the fund suffered that year. Nevertheless, it's only the second loss since 1994. Still, the percentage of foreign reserve to Hong Kong's GDP doubled from the level in 1997 during the Asian Financial Crisis. \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 25, 2016 03:05 ET (08:05 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160125ec1p000i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
