\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Shares Rise on ECB Stimulus Hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,186 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2016\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA         15882.68  115.94  0.74% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2         4472.06    0.37  0.01% \par S&P 500       1868.99    9.66  0.52% \par FTSE 100      5773.79  100.21  1.77% \par Nikkei Stock 16533.07  515.81  3.22% \par Hang Seng    18938.80  396.65  2.14% \par Kospi         1869.50   28.97  1.57% \par SGX Nifty     7366.00   60.00  0.82% \par Jan contract \par  \par USD/JPY  117.63-64   -0.07% \par Range    117.97   117.55 \par EUR/USD  1.0847-50   -0.24% \par Range    1.0878   1.0841 \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBOT\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wheat March  $4.750 per bushel \par Comex Gold        $1,098.20   -0.7% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude (NY)     $29.79  +$1.44 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks pushed higher, offering a reprieve from this week's rout, as oil prices climbed and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suggested it was willing to offer more stimulus at its next meeting in March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average climbed 116 points, or 0.7%, to 15882, after earlier rising as much as 272 points. The S&P 500 gained 0.5%, while the Nasdaq Composite edged up less than 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 The rally marked a break from the recent bout of selling that has sent stock markets around the world tumbling through their lows of last summer. The Dow has lost almost 9% this year and the S&P 500 fell Wednesday to its lowest level since 2014. Also Wednesday, Japan's Nikkei and the U.K.'s FTSE 100 index fell into bear-market territory, marked by a decline of at least 20% from recent highs.\par \par \uc2 Energy stocks posted some of the widest gains following the jump in oil prices. Energy companies in the S&P 500 gained 2.9%.\par \par \uc2 Still, many investors remain worried that there could be further declines to come. Concerns about China's slowing growth clip and the tumbling price of oil have led to sharp stock-market swings on a near daily basis.\par \par \uc2 "Healthy corrections are when you slowly climb back up on incremental good news after a sharp selloff," said John Toohey, head of equities at USAA Investments. That hasn't been the case. Rather than welcoming Thursday's bounce back, he said it makes him uneasy.\par \par \uc2 "Instead of a big one-day upswing, I would rather see several days in a row of small gains," he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most shares in Asia rallied on Friday after a volatile week, as investors put their hopes in more stimulus from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai Composite Index gained 1%, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was up 2.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average surged 3.4%, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was up 1.1% and South Korea's Kospi gained 1.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gains marked a break from recent selling that pushed Japan's stock market into bear-market territory earlier in the week, joining Hong Kong and China. A bear market is a drop of at least 20% from a recent high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Energy shares in the region rose as Brent oil prices rose 1% to $29.54 early in trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures logged their biggest one-day gains in three months overnight, with oil prices in New York gaining 4.2% to $29.53 a barrel on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Worries about weaker global growth had been reflected in new lows for oil prices earlier in the week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overnight, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the bank may need to review its policy stance at its next meeting, indicating the bank could be open to more stimulus measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In China, investors were assessing comments on the stock market by Chinese officials at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Economic Forum\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Davos, Switzerland. On Thursday, China's Vice President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li Yuanchao\f28 \uc2 \f2  signaled that Beijing would keep \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its stock market in an interview widely circulated by local media.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai Composite Index had plunged 3.2% the previous session, as widening losses in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and signs of lessening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Beijing spooked investors. The benchmark is down 18% year-to-date. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's daily USD/CNY benchmark rate was moved lower to 6.5572 from 6.5585, implying a 0.02% strengthening of the yuan versus the U.S. unit. The fractionally stronger yuan fixing marks the eleventh day in a row that the daily USD/CNY parity rate has barely changed. Recent volatility for the yuan has subsided, partly due to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restricting shorting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the offshore market, and partly due to the very stable daily yuan fixings. Some analysts though, are wary that this is the calm before the storm. Paranoid as it may be, it has been pointed out that the daily USD/CNY fixing was very stable from June till early August, shortly before the abrupt 1.8% devaluation of the yuan by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . As debateable as this theory may be, there is no denying that punters are still betting on yuan weakness, just not in the tightly controlled spot market. At 0153 GMT, USD/CNY was at 6.5797, unchanged from its Thursday close. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper futures rose Thursday on mixed copper supply-demand data and increased expectations of further European stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The most-actively traded March copper contract settled up 1.9% at $1.9965 a pound on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices surged to their biggest gains in months Thursday, briefly peaking over $30 a barrel, after traders learned that oil supplies failed to rise as quickly as the most extreme estimates had suggested.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude settled up $1.18, or 4.2%, at $29.53 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Brent, the global benchmark, gained $1.37, or 4.9%, to $29.25 a barrel on \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Futures Europe. Both markets had their biggest gains by percentage for any one day since October. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Draghi Hints at More Stimulus \par Facebook Criticized in India Over Free Limited Internet \par China's Working-Age Population Sees Biggest-Ever Decline \par U.S. Payment of $1.7 Billion to Iran Raises Questions of Ransom \par American Express to Cut $1 Billion in Costs \par Nigeria Seeks Emergency OPEC Meeting \par SABMiller Pressured by Volatile \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par Starbucks Gives Soft Profit Outlook \par Boeing to Cut Production of 747s \par Court Declines to Block EPA Carbon Rule During Litigation \par Verizon Swings to a Profit \par E*Trade's Earnings More Than Double \par Cerberus Executive Defends Deal With Avon--2nd Update \par Japan Road Tests Self-Driving Cars to Keep Aging Motorists Mobile \par Egypt Moves to Head Off Popular Unrest \par Judge Encourages Plaintiff to Drop GM Ignition-Switch Lawsuit \par J.P. Morgan CEO's 2015 Pay Up $7 Million From Prior Year \par Fears of Venezuela Default Grow Amid Oil Plunge \par Worker Strikes Against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart\f28 \uc2 \f2  In 2013 Were Lawful, NLRB Judge Says \par AmEx Offers Gloomy Outlook as Profit Drops 38% \par Charney Fights \f28 \uc2 \f2 American Apparel\f28 \uc2 \f2  Exit Plan in Court \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 21, 2016 22:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160122ec1m000fo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
