\par\par \b \uc2 China shares follow Asian bourses lower as oil rally fizzles\b0\par\par\uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2016\par 11:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Reuters) - China's fragile stock markets ended sharply lower on Thursday as oil prices failed to sustain a bounce from 13-year lows and other Asian markets went into reverse.\par \par \uc2 With tumbling oil prices indicative of slowing global growth, in which China plays a central part, the benchmark Shanghai Composite Index ended down 3.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 That followed a morning of up and down trade as the market lived up to its reputation for volatility, with moves amplified by low volumes.\par \par \uc2 The index has slumped more than 18 percent in 2016 as China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under pressure and economic indicators have confirmed its declining growth, putting the world's second-largest economy at the foreground of global investors' concerns.\par \par \uc2 At this level, investors who bought back into the market at the lowest point after regulators arrested a 40 percent crash last summer are back to square one.\par \par \uc2 The CSI300 index of the largest listed companies in Shanghai and Shenzhen shed 2.9 percent, taking its losses for the year to more than 17 percent.\par \par \uc2 Lu Jie, head of China research at Robeco Asia Investment Center, said there was a vicious cycle in the markets.\par \par \uc2 "China falls trigger declines in overseas markets, which in turn fuel further slides in China markets. Panic has been amplified," he said.\par \par \uc2 However, he said the market could stabilize around March, when Chinese leaders are expected to unveil policies to support the economy at the National People's Congress, and believed that 2,800 points could be the SSEC's floor for this year.\par \par \uc2 Shi Wei, who manages roughly 4 billion yuan ($608 million) at Shanghai-based hedge fund Broad Capital, said ample liquidity would prevent an extended bear market, but a poor economy means there's not much room to go up either.\par \par \uc2 "The investment strategy this year is to be brave enough to buy during slumps and quick to take profit during rebounds," he said.\par \par \uc2 China has come in for criticism that it has left markets confused about its policies, and occasionally its stated intentions such as keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable have not always matched its actions.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  in Davos, International Monetary Fund director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  said China needed to communicate better with financial markets.\par \par \uc2 "There is a communication issue, which markets do not like," she said.\par \par \uc2 Fang Xinghai, vice chairman of the securities regulator, also at Davos, said China was learning to "communicate seamlessly" with the markets, which he said ought to do more to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 YUAN STEADY\par \par \uc2 The yuan has had a volatile start to 2016, after depreciating about 5 percent since August.\par \par \uc2 After jolting global markets by allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slide sharply early in January, the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 has kept a steady course for the yuan's daily midpoint fix in the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's fix was barely changed at 6.5585 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan is under depreciation pressure, but China has the ability to control its pace, and indeed the yuan has already stabilized," said David Dai, Shanghai-based investor director at Nanhai Fund Management Co.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed that bearish positions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have eased a little in the last two weeks, deterred by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determined \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the spot market, the yuan was at 6.5788, just a few pips softer than Wednesday's close, while offshore it was nearly 0.5 percent weaker at 6.6084.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also generous with liquidity ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday by injecting a net 315 billion yuan ($48 billion) into the banking system for the week to avoid a cash crunch during the long holiday.\par \par \uc2 It was the biggest weekly injection since January 2014, and came on top of other recent \uc2 PBOC\uc2  moves to avert any undue strains in coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG SPILLOVER\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has acted aggressively to deter speculators from shorting the yuan, also known as the renminbi (RMB), but the yuan's bumpy fall in the past six months, heavy capital outflows and a cooling economy have reinforced market expectations that something will have to give.\par \par \uc2 Speculators have taken to using the yuan's cheaper offshore forwards market to wager China will finally devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around March or April.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Thursday that net capital outflows had eased in the fourth quarter last year, and risks were under control.\par \par \uc2 Hao Hong, Managing Director at Research BOCOM International, said the consequences of China's attempts to manage its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and support its stocks were spilling over into \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mainland assets such as the RMB and A-shares have prevented market prices from adjusting toward China\u8217\'20\'19s deteriorating fundamentals, and forced volatility to manifest in Hong Kong assets," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar fell to its lowest in more than eight years on Wednesday as the market tested the city's long-standing peg to the U.S. dollar, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng share index, however, dropped another 1.5 percent to plumb lows not seen since August 2012.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 6.5750 Chinese yuan renminbi)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Pete Sweeney, Samuel Shen and Shanghai and Beijing newsrooms; Writing by Wayne Cole and Will Waterman; Editing by Kim Coghill & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-21T10:17:52.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160120ec1k002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
