\par\par \b \uc2 China Markets Dive on Pessimism Over Falling Hong Kong Shares -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2016\par 08:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The selloff accelerated in China markets Thursday, as widening losses on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and signs of lessening government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  spooked investors. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index slumped 3.2% at 2880.48, and the Shenzhen Composite Index dived 4% at 1800.99. Resource, steel and brokerage shares dived, dragging down the Shanghai index. Over 2,700 stocks retreat in Shanghai and Shenzhen today, with 91 stocks falling by the daily maximum of 10%. Markets in Hong Kong retreated in the afternoon session, with the Hang Seng Index falling 1.4%. "The sudden retreat of the Hong Kong market intensified worries that it hasn't bottomed out yet," says Shen Zhengyang, analyst at Northeast Securities. In addition, the liquidity injection by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  virtually wiped out hope that there might be another reserve ratio cut by the Lunar New Year.\par \par \uc2  Mood may also turn more pessimistic after China's securities official Fang Xinghai said at Davos today that the world should get used to volatility of the A-share market and that the government may reduce market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .(yifan.xie@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0728 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan's Taiex edges 0.6% lower to 7664.01 on Thursday with thin turnover of NT$72.8 billion. Financial and industrial shares were hammered following poor export data, but strength in the tech sector helped offset losses. In a 9-month shrinking streak, Taiwan's December export orders fell 12.4% year over year. The sharp fall is disappointing against the expected 7% contraction, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding that the island's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely wait for more data before making a move. Financial holding companies \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cathay\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2882.TW) and Fubon (2881.TW) decline 1% and 2%, respectively. Tech heavyweight \f28 \uc2 \f2 Largan Precision\f28 \uc2 \f2  (3008.TW) surges 7.1% to NT$2035. Communication-equipment makers \f28 \uc2 \f2 CyberTAN Technology\f28 \uc2 \f2  (3062.TW), Hitron Technologies (2419.TW) and Edimax (3047.TW) as well as game developer X-Legend Entertainment (4994.TW) soar by the 10% daily trading limit. (john.wu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TAG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0719 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese stocks ended down, led by constant futures-selling in the afternoon trading, says Yusuke Sakai, senior trader at T&D Asset Management. He says commodity trading advisors or other short-term speculators tend to reverse trades after making bets for a certain period of time. Recent continued selling in stocks suggest the sellers may be those who wish to offload real stock holdings, he says. The Nikkei ended down 2.4% at 16017.26. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0700 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan Post Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7182.TO) Executive Vice President Katsunori Sago says that he wants to shift the culture of the bank to encourage individuals to bring forward ideas and contribute to the firm's performance, and that an incentive-based pay system is a good way to do that. "We have to create a culture where innovative ideas are raised up within the organization," he says. He says that he wants to create a compensation system at the bank that will "attract talent capable of investing 200 trillion yen ($1.7 trillion)." He says he would be open to hiring foreign investment staff, but that Japanese ability would be necessary at the firm's current stage. (eleanor.warnock@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0656 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks prices of pulses--grain legumes that include chickpeas and pigeon peas--in India will fall soon, giving enough room to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  to cut rates. Pulses' prices have grown at a double-digit pace earlier in 2015 with pulse inflation soaring to about a 46% on-year rise in the last few months, says the broker. In turn, this has pushed inflation up. "With higher prices now producing a surge in imports, our base case is that pulse prices will now begin to pull back rapidly also in line with the experience of their last three supercycles," BNP says. Falling pulses prices will help in the Reserve Bank being able to keep to its Consumer Price Inflation target of close 5% in early 2017, providing room for rate cuts. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0655 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's rupee retreated and is now weaker on a daily basis despite chalking up early gains against the U.S. dollar. The USD/INR is back above the 68.00 trading barrier after falling as low as 67.82 on a broad recovery in market sentiment early Thursday. The relief rally for Asian stock markets, in the wake of Wall Street's rebound from its August-low overnight, fizzled as China's benchmark index struggled to recover after opening 1.4% lower. The Shanghai Composite, now down 1.3%, dragged the risk-sensitive Australian dollar lower. Risk aversion has hence returned, causing rupee weakness. The USD/INR is likely to head up in the near term and could reach a new two-year high if it surpasses Wednesday's peak of 68.17. The USD/INR is at 68.06 from its previous close of 67.95. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: FLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0648 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2  has achieved more progress in changing Japan in the last couple of years than successive governments did over the previous 20 years, says Templeton Global Macro Chief Investment Officer Michael Hasenstab. Despite growing criticism that Abe has been slow at carrying out structural reforms to raise Japan's growth potential, "the fact the Prime Minister Abe has been able to move himself into a position even to address this is a huge feat, because every prime minister before him was not even able to get that process started," Hasenstab tells The Wall Street Journal. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's quantitative easing and other steps have kept real interest rates low, weakened the yen, boosted risk assets and helped exports. "I think the policy mix is right from the macro standpoint," he says, though it is "an open question" whether Abe can follow through on other reforms, building on that success. kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0646 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Philippine peso reversed its initial rally and has fallen to a new six-year low of 47.95, surpassing yesterday's trough of 47.89 compared with the U.S. dollar. The tentative risk-on relief rally that helped most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  briefly recover from Wednesday's losses has ended. The trigger may have been the failed attempt by China's Shanghai Composite index, the region's equity benchmark, to recover after opening 1.4% lower. The Shanghai Composite drifted back down to its current loss of 1.2% after a blip higher, in the process overturning the rally of the risk-sensitive Australian dollar, and confirming the return of risk aversion. USD/PHP is likely to spike if it breaks through the 48.00 round-figure trading barrier--above which stop-loss orders are likely lurking. USD/PHP is now 47.91 from its last close of 47.89. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0645 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indian shares erase early gains to move lower as fall in energy and auto stocks weigh. The S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sensex declines 0.4% to 23,965.72 after rising as much as 1.2% in early trade. The National Stock Exchange's Nifty 50 index also slips 0.4% to 7,283.50. Analysts say investors are cautious amid global uncertainties and weak domestic macro data. Continuous selling by foreign investors, who hold significant shares in large companies, is also hurting the market. Foreign investors have sold $1.06 billion worth of Indian stocks so far this month. Among the major losers, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tata Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500570.BY) declines 4.3%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Maruti Suzuki\f28 \uc2 \f2  (532500.BY) falls 2.8%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oil & Natural Gas Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (500312.BY) is down 2.5%. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0634 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Luxury goods had a difficult year in Asia given the slowing Chinese economy, the ongoing crackdown against conspicuous consumption and fewer tourists traveling to Hong Kong. But most analysts don't see a collapse for the retailers. since there is strong Chinese travel elsewhere and Chinese tourists still have an appetite for French leather and silk. Travel trends, heightened safety concerns and a prolonged stock market rout will be more important drivers of growth for luxury this year than the Chinese economy or the level of the yuan, says Erwan Rambourg, global co-head of consumer and retail at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Chinese consumers haven't lost their appetite for luxury, rather they are evolving and are shopping less to show off status, he says. The psyche of the Chinese consumer will be more important driver of luxury consumption than economic growth, the bank argues.(anjie.zheng@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0624 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY (now at 116.84) may regain ground toward 118 once U.S. stocks show signs of recovering, says Koji Fukaya, chief executive at FPG Securities. "It looks like the dollar has found solid (new) support just below Y116," says Fukaya, adding that U.S. stocks will be a key factor in any upside momentum for the dollar. Still, Fukaya says the pair may hover in a 116-120 range for the next two to three months, as it may take time for U.S. stocks to track on an upward trajectory even after showing signs of bottoming out. The DJIA Average tumbled by more than 500 points overnight before rebounding to close down 249 points, or 1.6%. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 21 Jan 2016 02:38 ET \par China Markets Dive on Pessimism Over Falling Hong -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0613 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Despite the yen's recent renewed strength, Templeton Global Macro is betting against the yen, says chief investment officer Michael Hasenstab. Templeton Global Macro is a fixed-income fund with $150 billion of assets. He says the recent flight to quality flows on the back of global economic uncertainty will likely reverse, once such fears ease. "It is true that the global economy is slowing, but it is not collapsing. The markets are telling you it's collapsing," he says in an interview. An expected widening of the yield differential between the U.S. Treasurys and Japanese government bonds will also likely help weaken the yen against the dollar, as the U.S. tightens its monetary policy and Japan keeps easing. Reduced buying of U.S. Treasurys from oil producing countries is also likely to result in higher U.S. rates, says Hasenstab. The yen has gained 2.9% against the dollar so far this year. The USD/JPY is at 116.96. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 21, 2016 02:38 ET (07:38 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160121ec1l000ls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
