\par\par \b \uc2 Should We Worry About The Hong Kong Dollar? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2016\par 04:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Winter is coming to Hong Kong. The Hong Kong dollar breached its 2007 low today, down to as low as 7.8226, just haircuts away from the 7.85 level that would prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After China decoupled its loosely pegged yuan from the dollar last August, we Hong Kong residents are understandably worried Hong Kong may de-peg its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 But really, rather than the Hong Kong dollar, we should worry about the Hong Kong economy instead.\par \par \uc2 First of all, it is highly unlikely Hong Kong would want to rock the boat even though Hong Kong's economy is more closely tied to China (and so should its monetary policy be). After all, this is the government that lets its citizens kidnapped across the border without consequences.\par \par \uc2 Second, HKMA has enough gun power to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it comes to it. Hong Kong's foreign reserve is currently at $359 billion, which covers 1.75 times its monetary base. See my last week's blog for \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's commentary on the possibility of Hong Kong de-pegging.\par \par \uc2 But what this means is that Hong Kong has to raise its interest rates to compensate for the Hong Kong dollar outflow, estimated to be around 300 billion Hong Kong dollars, or $38 billion. This certainly is not good news for the Hong Kong economy, especially when it is already in the downturn. In 2015, Hong Kong retail sales, a growth engine in recent years, is expected to slump over 5%, even worse than the SARs epidemic in 2003.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong investors are catching up to reality today, sending the Hang Seng Index down 3.1% a new 40-month low. No surprise, Hong Kong property developers tumbled today. Cheung Kong Property ( 1113.Hong Kong) fell 5.6%, Wheelock ( 20.Hong Kong) dropped 4.8%, Wharf Holding ( 4.Hong Kong) was down 3.7%.\par \par \uc2 Year-to-date, the \uc2 iShares China Large-Cap ETF\uc2  (FXI) fell 13.5%, the \uc2 iShares MSCI China ETF\uc2  (MCHI) fell 13.4%, the iShares MSCI Hong Kong ETF (EWH) was down 10%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 19, 2016 22:04 ET (03:04 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160120ec1k0005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
