\par\par \b \uc2 CNH TRACKER-Dim sum bond sale commissions shrink, volume halves in 2015\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Chen   \par \uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2016\par 04:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 14 (Reuters) - Yuan bond sale underwriting fees in the offshore market shrank last year as Chinese issuers found cheaper funding on the mainland while foreign buyers stayed away due to yuan weakness and volatility.\par \par \uc2 The offshore debt market saw its first annual primary issuance decline since it kicked off nearly a decade ago, with issuance more than halving in 2015 from the previous year.\par \par \uc2 Banks received an estimated $51.2 million from underwriting dim sum bonds last year, down 62 percent from $136 million recorded a year earlier, according to Thomson Reuters/Freeman Consulting.\par \par \uc2 The average commission per deal also fell to $0.13 million last year from $0.22 million, the statistics showed.\par \par \uc2 "Underwriting fees this year can be even lower than last year as the dim sum bond supply is expected to fall further and commission rates also have room to be squeezed," said a debt capital market banker at a Chinese bank in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "We will not recommend the dim sum bond market to our clients who plan to raise funds unless they want to, but switch our focus to the dollar bond market," the banker said.\par \par \uc2 Dim sum bond issuance fell to 163 billion yuan ($24.8 billion) in 2015, less than half of that sold a year earlier, \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  data shows. The slide was heaviest in the second half of the year as China markets crashed and Beijing unexpectedly devalued the yuan in early August.\par \par \uc2 The debt market is still facing strong headwinds this year given demand will continue to be curbed as foreign investors sell off yuan assets amid weak economic fundamentals in China and on expectations the yuan is set to weaken further.\par \par \uc2 Chinese issuers that used to be key players in the market mostly disappeared last year after funding costs on the mainland fell much lower than in Hong Kong, especially for high-yield issuers such as property firms.\par \par \uc2 A 10-year Chinese government dim sum bond fetched a yield of 3.7 percent on Thursday, compared with 2.8 percent for its counterpart onshore.\par \par \uc2 The trend appears set to continue this year with the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 expected to maintain a loose monetary policy to shore up the sluggish economy, while yuan liquidity remains tight in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Implied overnight interest rates for China's yuan hit a record high of 94 percent in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, as suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  drained liquidity in the offshore market.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the weak sentiment in the dim sum bond market, it may see some technical support this year as there will not be much supply," said a fixed income fund manager in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The total return of dim sum bonds in U.S. dollar terms slid to -3.2 percent in 2015, the worst level since the market came into being, \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 WEEK IN REVIEW:\par \par \uc2 * China has allowed a second batch of foreign institutions to enter China's interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, including \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of International Settlements\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  are negotiating on a resumption of a bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap arrangement in case of a financial crisis, sources with knowledge of the matter told Reuters on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 * China's offshore yuan firmed above the onshore rate on Tuesday morning for the first time since October, as traders pointed to state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate arbitrage strategies for pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  abruptly together. ]\par \par \uc2 * China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator has ordered banks in some of the country's major import and export centres to limit purchases of U.S. dollars this month, three people with direct knowledge have told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 * Bearish bets on the yuan hit a near six-year high, denting sentiment towards most other emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate at a faster pace, a Reuters poll showed.\par \par \uc2 CHART OF THE WEEK:\par \par \uc2 Dim sum bond issuance has lost momentum: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://graphics.thomsonreuters.com/dimsum/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://graphics.thomsonreuters.com/dimsum/index.html}}}\par \par \uc2 RECENT STORIES: Hong Kong yuan deposits under heavy pressure as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wilts China winning offshore yuan battle, but may be losing the war\par \par \uc2 \f2 More stories about the CNH market                 \par Daily onshore yuan reports                        \par Daily China money market reports                  \par   \par Offshore yuan rate    Onshore yuan rate  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Offshore yuan dealt Onshore yuan on CFETS THOMSON REUTERS SPEED GUIDES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ($1 = 6.5747 Chinese yuan) (Editing by Jacqueline Wong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-14T04:42:46.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160114ec1e005bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex10