\par\par \b \uc2 Beijing Flexes Muscle to Put Yuan in Line\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anjani Trivedi and Fiona Law \par \uc2 1,284 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2016\par 03:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--Beijing escalated the battle for control of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , effectively shutting down the offshore market where traders had been betting on a decline in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Traders said a surge of yuan buying by state-owned banks on Friday and Monday in Hong Kong sent the cost of borrowing yuan overnight soaring past 66% by Tuesday, making it prohibitively expensive for investors to finance bets against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known as a short sale. The move has boosted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value by as much as 1.8% since Friday against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Beijing typically enlists state-owned financial firms to do its bidding and has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore market in the past.\par \par \uc2 The show of force followed strong warnings from Chinese officials and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days that the country wouldn't let \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation spiral out of control.\par \par \uc2 The government hopes to gradually weaken the yuan to help stimulate the country's slowing growth, according to interviews with Chinese officials and advisers to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it is a perilous effort that risks being overrun by traders trying to get ahead of the depreciation.\par \par \uc2 China is sensitive about signals being sent by the $230 billion-a-day offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, the main center for free trade in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The gap between the value of the yuan in Hong Kong and in mainland China widened to a record last week, as offshore traders bet the country would need to further weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a pair of jarring depreciations.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  sets the yuan's rate against the dollar in the onshore, or domestic, market, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed to trade 2% above or below that level. In the offshore market, principally Hong Kong, its value floats freely.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as much as 2.7% last week in Hong Kong, much deeper than the 1.1% decline the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  had guided in the onshore market, leading to speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was losing its touch. Inside China, consumers rushed to banks to buy dollars and sell yuan, also known as the renminbi.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to reassert who is boss. In recent days, its officials have dialed up their attacks on the people they blame for the yuan's earlier decline, branding them "speculators."\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \uc2 Han Jun\uc2  , deputy director of the office of the Central Leading Group on Financial and Economic Affairs, said market bets that the yuan could slump by 10% or more against the dollar were "ridiculous and impossible." He confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore market on Friday and Monday.\par \par \uc2 "Attempts to sell short the renminbi will not succeed," Mr. Han, a senior aide in President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  's economic advisory group, told an audience in New York. "The expectations of markets can be changed."\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had pointed to "speculative forces" that were driving its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, saying the fall had "nothing to do with [China's] real economy."\par \par \uc2 Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have consistently defended the way they are managing the yuan, saying it has remained largely stable against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even when it fell against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Priorities for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have shifted for Beijing. Last year, its main effort was to cast the yuan as a stable, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  added the yuan to its basket of global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November--part of Beijing's efforts to expand its use world-wide--officials shifted their goals to using the yuan to stimulate the economy, according to Chinese officials and advisers to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can help an economy by boosting inflation and exports.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have overshot in that effort and is now resorting to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to regain control.\par \par \uc2 It was the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own actions that triggered a selloff in the offshore yuan market in the first trading days of 2016.\par \par \uc2 From Monday to Thursday last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the onshore rate significantly weaker against the dollar, sparking fears it was more worried about the health of the Chinese economy than it had previously let on.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted tack and began propping up the swiftly declining yuan beginning Friday, when they saw large state-owned Chinese banks step in to sell greenbacks and buy the yuan in overseas markets. The offshore yuan strengthened as much as 1.5% late Monday against the dollar and continued to advance Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Chinese banks' buying spree, seen as a sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , effectively shut down the yuan market in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, as supply of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  all but disappeared. The benchmark rate at which banks borrow yuan from each other surged to an unprecedented 66.8%, up from what had been a record level of 13.4% on Monday. The benchmark is based on rates contributed by 18 domestic banks each morning. Traders said the actual yuan borrowing rate reached as high as 110% during the day.\par \par \uc2 "There's no cash in the market," said a senior trader at a bank in Hong Kong, referring to the yuan's availability.\par \par \uc2 The rise in borrowing costs has made it much more expensive for investors to pursue trading strategies that bet against the yuan, said Khoon Goh, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group Ltd\uc2  . in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 As the cost of funding for such bets rose Tuesday, investors tried to close remaining trades, fearing deeper losses later if they didn't act soon.\par \par \uc2 The retreat allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to achieve its aim of bringing its value inside and outside China back in line, a shift that restricts traders from profiting from the differences between the yuan's offshore and onshore value in the future. As the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened in offshore markets Tuesday, at one point it traded at a premium to its onshore counterpart.\par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday, the yuan traded onshore closed at 6.5695 against the U.S. dollar, and the offshore yuan was at 6.5746.\par \par \uc2 "It's so far the biggest signal from the [\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ]," a Hong Kong-based senior trader at a Wall Street bank said of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts, while effective for now, could come at both a short- and long-term cost.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions raise concerns over Beijing policy makers' pledges to give markets a bigger say over the yuan. Investors in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the offshore market could hinder the yuan's development as a widely traded global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This has had a quite a dramatic impact on the market," said Alexandra Edstein, a portfolio manager at hedge fund the Cambridge Strategy. "Whether it lasts is the question."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the rise in interbank borrowing costs in Hong Kong, engineered to support the yuan, could crimp new lending by banks that don't have enough yuan deposits and need to borrow, said \uc2 Michael Wu\uc2 , senior equity analyst at Morningstar Investment Management Asia. Hong Kong banks already have been paring back their yuan lending businesses as China's growth slowed, he said.\par \par \uc2 Also, the recent turbulence in yuan markets could keep the trading and issuance of offshore yuan-denominated bonds, dubbed dim-sum bonds, subdued, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Julie Steinberg and Lingling Wei contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anjani Trivedi at anjani.trivedi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 13-01-16 0218GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160113ec1d0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\p