\par\par \b \uc2 China Tumble Leads Asian Markets Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2016\par 01:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China shares plunged Monday, approaching their low reached during the summer crash, as regulators appeared to refrain from propping up the market.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index fell 5.3% to 3016.70, the lowest finish for the benchmark in more than three months. It has shed 15% so far this year. Another 3% drop, or 89 points, would put the benchmark below its close of 2927.29 reached on Aug. 26. At that point, Shanghai had lost more than 40% from its June peak.\par \par \uc2 The smaller Shenzhen Composite Index fell 6.6% to 1848.10.\par \par \uc2 Shares in Hong Kong sank to their lowest in roughly 2\u189\'00\'BD years. The Hang Seng Index was off 2.8% at 19888.50, closing below 20000 for the first time since June 2013. A gauge of Chinese firms listed in the city fell 3.9%.\par \par \uc2 Australia's benchmark S&P/ASX 200 lost 1.2% and South Korea's Kospi fell 1.2%. Japan's market was closed for a national holiday.\par \par \uc2 Traders said state-owned funds were notably absent from the market Monday. Buying by China's so called "National Team" had helped stabilize the market last summer and, at times, late last year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lack of aggressive easing measures over the weekend to boost the economy also disappointed investors. While some say they still expect officials to take action, including further cuts to interest rates and the amount of reserves banks are required to hold, a lack of clear communication from authorities is worrying.\par \par \uc2 "[All] this may have conveyed a sense of sanguinity from regulators and the government regarding recent market volatility," said Bill Bowler, equities sales trader at Hong Kong-based research and brokerage firm Forsyth Barr Asia Ltd.\par \par \uc2 Poorly telegraphed moves last week exacerbated volatility in China and triggered selling that spread to the rest of the region, the U.S. and Europe\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, worries about weakness in the Chinese yuan and how authorities convey their market expectations continue to unnerve investors, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilized in the past two days.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's weakening by 1.5% against the U.S. dollar last week sparked selling in commodities and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China's trading partners and sent investors to assets perceived as safe.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Monday, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the yuan at 6.5626 against the dollar, guiding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger from its 6.5636 fixing on Friday. It was the second day the bank guided the yuan stronger, after eight sessions of weaker guidance. The onshore yuan can trade up or down 2% from the fix.\par \par \uc2 The onshore yuan was last at 6.5833 against the U.S. dollar, compared with 6.5938 late Friday. It reached a five-year low of 6.5956 last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan, which trades freely, was last at 6.6727 to the U.S. dollar, compared with 6.6820 late Friday. It reached a five-year low of 6.7511 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have spent huge amounts of dollars to support the yuan amid decelerating economic growth and the onset of higher U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said over the weekend that its reserves are "relatively sufficient." Reserves dropped by $107.9 billion in December, the biggest monthly drop ever.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, the short-term rate Hong Kong banks charge each other to borrow Chinese yuan jumped to a record 13.4% from 4% on Friday, an indication that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil is spreading to \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The increase comes as traders said large Chinese banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  offshore to buy yuan last week, effectively tightening liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Worries about slackening demand in China, one of the world's biggest consumers of commodities from oil to copper, trammeled prices early Monday. Copper fell to a six-and-a-half year low of $4,416 a ton before recovering to $4,485.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil was down 3% at $32.91 a barrel. The global oil benchmark has fallen more than 13% year-to-date amid China worries and Middle East tensions.\par \par \uc2 The plethora of reasons for global unease\u8212\'20\'14from China's slowdown to tensions in the Middle East and North Korea's nuclear test\u8212\'20\'14has sent investors toward haven assets.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen last traded at \u165\'00\'A5 117.12 per U.S. dollar, 0.3% stronger than its level late Friday in Asia and its strongest in about four months.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were up 0.7% at $1,105.10 a troy ounce. Prices have risen about 4% year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 Gregor Stuart Hunter and Fiona Law contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 10, 2016 19:35 ET (00:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160111ec1b0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
