\par\par \b \uc2 China c.bank suspected to have  \highlight22\uc2 intervened \highlight\uc2  to support yuan-traders\b0\par\par\uc2 175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2016\par 03:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 /SHANGHAI, Jan 5 (Reuters) - China's onshore yuan  firmed against the dollar on Tuesday after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , three traders told Reuters.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "It's quite obvious that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market via big Chinese banks in the morning and trading was very active," said a trader at a Chinese bank in Shanghai.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The yuan closed at 6.5338 per dollar on Monday, but strengthend immediately after the opening on Tuesday and traded at 6.5197 by mid-morning.\par \par \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets had plunged sharply on Monday in response to weak economic data, sparking a global sell-off of riskier assets.\par \par \par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually takes a hands-off attitude, the yuan remains weak and hit 6.6446 in early trade, the lowest level in more than four years.\par \par \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Michelle Chen and Lu Jianxin; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-8T02:41:29.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160105ec15003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
