\par\par \b \uc2 China Must Speed Up Financial Reform, Says \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Official\b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2015\par 10:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING\u8212\'20\'14China must intensify its efforts to open up its financial system to the outside world despite the country's recent market volatility, a senior \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official warned, in an apparent effort to shore up support for reforming China's vast but creaky financial plumbing.\par \par \uc2 Sheng Songcheng, head of the survey and statistics department at the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , said at a forum in Shanghai on Friday that there is "no direct connection" between volatility in China's financial markets and China's recent effort to give foreign investors greater access. Foreign ownership accounts for only 2.5% of Chinese stocks, Mr. Sheng said, according to a copy of his remarks reviewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, slowing down the pace of freeing cross-border money flows would only "prolong the uncertainty" over China's commitment to reforms, he said. Mr. Sheng said stalled reform would encourage differences in interest rates and exchange rates between China and other countries, encouraging speculators.\par \par \uc2 "Reform should be sped up," Mr. Sheng added.\par \par \uc2 The comments come as \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fighting to stick with a year-end timetable it set for greater openness in the country's markets. Following a stock-market meltdown in the summer and recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, some officials within the Chinese bureaucracy are raising concerns over continued opening at a time of persistent economic weakness and growing worries about financial instability.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, PBOC Gov. \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  pledged greater openness for the financial system of the world's No. 2 economy by the end of 2015. \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2 , a deputy PBOC governor responsible for day-to-day oversight of China's financial flows, said in late August that China would move forward with opening its markets up to foreign investors according to its timetable.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sheng reiterated that sentiment on Friday, saying reform "shouldn't be put off because of market volatility." Rather, freer movements of money across China's borders could help smooth out the fluctuations, he said.\par \par \uc2 For instance, Mr. Sheng said, the Chinese yuan currently trades at different values inside and outside the country. That creates problems for regulators, as some investors seek to profit from the difference and as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to forge a unified exchange rate in its bid to get the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to declare the yuan an official reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan trades both in the domestic market and in what is known as the offshore market in Hong Kong, a Chinese city which operates under its own laws. In mainland China, authorities strictly limit the way the yuan trades. In Hong Kong, it can be bought and sold freely. Since Aug. 11, when China surprised the world by devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , investors in Hong Kong have been pounding the yuan weaker in expectation of more devaluation to come. The offshore yuan has traded at a discount of 1% or more to its mainland cousin at times since then, while the two were largely in line before the devaluation.\par \par \uc2 That widened spread is complicating China's ambitions to raise the yuan's global profile. Beijing is trying to persuade the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to include the yuan in its elite basket of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , giving it the same status as the U.S. dollar, the euro, the British pound and the Japanese yen. But people familiar with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's thinking say it is unlikely to deem a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with two exchange rates "freely usable," as the organization requires.\par \par \uc2 To narrow the gap, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  in recent weeks has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in both the mainland and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bring the onshore and offshore yuan more in line with each other.\par \par \uc2 If China sufficiently opens the capital account and gives foreign investors easier access to the mainland market, Mr. Sheng said, the division between the offshore and onshore yuan would be reduced significantly.\par \par \uc2 He added that China remains attractive to long-term foreign capital, adding that foreign direct investment in China increased $1.51 billion year-over-year to $8.71 billion in August.\par \par \uc2 Grace Zhu.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 25, 2015 04:35 ET (08:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150925eb9p000os\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
