\par\par \b \uc2 China  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  seen ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  again if offshore pushes yuan too far\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Chen   \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2015\par 12:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Reuters) - A spike higher in the offshore yuan following suspected rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Chinese state banks is expected to be short-lived, especially with a looming U.S. interest rate rise likely to add to the attraction of owning dollars.\par \par \uc2 But those betting on a further depreciation in the yuan are likely to have only limited room to push the offshore rate down relative to the onshore rate without drawing the ire of the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the risk of further state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market sources said.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not stand aside if depreciation expectation is formed again and more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may happen," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 -based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, who declined to be identified.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan <CNH=D3> shot up by more than 1 percent on Thursday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was seen by traders as a gesture by authorities to shake out speculators betting against the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Authorities have spent the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves heavily to hold the yuan steady onshore since a surprise devaluation in August prompted fears the Chinese economy was in worse shape than previously thought and that the yuan therefore could fall further.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's spike narrowed the offshore yuan's discount against the onshore rate to 0.38 percent from 1.56 percent on Wednesday and forced traders with short positions to cover. Traders suspect the sudden move was prompted by buying by state banks at the behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purpose was to narrow the gap between the offshore and onshore rates, but I don't see any fundamental that supports yuan appreciation against the dollar going forward," said Penny Chen, a fixed-income fund manager at Manulife Asset Management in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Since the Aug 11 devaluation, several investment banks, including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and \uc2 UBS\uc2 , have revised down their forecasts for the yuan's performance this year.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain under pressure as long as U.S. interest rates are set to rise, analysts said. U.S. market rates have already risen in anticipation the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise its policy rate by the end of this year for the first time since 2006.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut lending rates five times since last November and analysts expect further easing of monetary policy to support China's economy.\par \par \uc2 The interest rate differential will be reflected more in the offshore market, where \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has less influence, analysts said. The offshore yuan was quoted at 6.4150 per dollar late on Friday in Asia, compared with an onshore rate of 6.3740 - a gap of 410 pips. It was 200 pips earlier in the day, but traders said the gap widened after London offshore markets kicked into action.\par \par \uc2 Some traders believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not allow the gap to widen to 500 pips for fear that would strengthen expectations of further yuan depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has less influence in the offshore market, which is more driven by market forces, and so leaves more room for the yuan to fall, said Liao Qun, China chief economist at Citic Bank International in Hong Kong. "The gap between CNH (offshore) and CNY (onshore) will be widened to 500-600 pips again in the coming months," Liao said.\par \par \uc2 Liao expected the onshore and offshore rates to fall to 6.45 and to more than 6.5 per dollar, respectively, by the end of the year if the Fed increases its benchmark rates.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Saikat Chatterjee; Editing by Neil Fullick) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-11T10:08:45.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150911eb9b00aq6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
