\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE How Much Did China Spend Defending The Yuan? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2015\par 05:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 After moving to a more market-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime on August 11, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  let the yuan go for only three days and has been trying to prop up the yuan by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling its dollar reserves.\par \par \uc2 $150 billion, estimates \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , is the amount China spent in August. For comparison purposes, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $50 billion in July, itself a monthly record.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, China has a deep pocket. It held $3.65 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as of the end of July.\par \par \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 's Wei Yao and Jason Daw admitted their estimate was very rough. The actual amount can be anywhere between $100 and $200 billion.\par \par \uc2 If the actual amount turned out to be $100 billion, then the PBoC may be asking its state-owned commercial banks to do the dirty work:\par \par \uc2 If the PBoC's figure surprises on the upside (ie. less decline than expected), one possible reason could be that state banks, policy banks or SOE's bought RMB at the behest of the PBoC and kept the risk on their balance sheet. Capital controls may have also tightened for corporates and households.\par \par \uc2 Nice. Is this partly why China's banks are now even cheaper than before? See also my Septemtber 2 blog " Value Trap: China Banks Report The Worst Earnings In A Decade".\par \par \uc2 Well, consider the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  a beneficiary. The PBoC guided its yuan fix rate higher earlier this week - to make China look strong and steady ahead of the big military parade yesterday, many commentators said. Households and businesses took advantage of this window and rushed out of their yuan accounts to buy \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars. As a result, the HKMA had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice, buying over $3.5 billion U.S. dollars in two days with mint money to keep the Hong Kong dollar within the strong end of the 7.75 peg range. See also my blog yesterday " Will Hong Kong Keep The Dollar Peg?".\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan was trading at 6.4481 recently. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  re-iterated its year-end forecast of 6.80. Overnight, the Powershares DB US Dollar Index Bullish Fund ( UUP) rose 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 UPDATE: Every Wall Street bank is coming up with some estimates today. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 's estimate is on the high end. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's Zhiwei Zhang for instance thinks China spent $90 billion defending the yuan:\par \par \uc2 We estimate that the PBoC may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on 3 days since the RMB depreciation on August 11, with average daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some US$16bn. On other days the average daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be in the range of US$4bn to US$8bn, and it seems to have declined substantially in recent trading days. We believe the most intensive episode of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have passed. Monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the next few months will probably not exceed US$90bn.\par \par \uc2 Chill, people! The PBoC will announce the official number next Monday. We will find out then!\par \par \uc2 More at Barron's Asia Stocks to Watch blog, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/asiastocks/}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 04-09-15 0310GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150904eb940002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
