\par\par \b \uc2 Will Hong Kong Keep The Dollar Peg? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2015\par 08:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Wednesday to hold onto its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected 12.5 billion Hong Kong dollars, after buying HK$15.5 billion worth of U.S. dollars on Tuesday, the first time since April.\par \par \uc2 Under the current system, the Hong Kong dollar is pegged at 7.8 per U.S. dollar but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85. HKMA is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hit 7.75 or 7.85. The Hong Kong dollar was hitting the strong end of 7.75 this week as businesses and households move out of their yuan deposits and into Hong Kong dollar accounts, expecting the yuan to depreciate further.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  devalued the yuan three weeks ago, markets have been speculating that Hong Kong will abandon the dollar peg. An expensive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hurting the island's retail sales. In July - even before the yuan devaluation - retail sales fell 2.8% from a year ago, sharpening from June's 0.4% decline. In addition, since the Hong Kong dollar is pegged, its monetary policy follows the US as well. As the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank\uc2  is on track to raise interest rates, cost of capital in Hong Kong will spike up too, exerting pressure on the local economy.\par \par \uc2 But economists think that Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't give up the dollar peg easily.\par \par \uc2 First, global trade growth is already anemic. The dollar peg reduces exporters' exchange rate risk. "In a small, highly trade-dependent economy, with exports the equivalent of over 200% of GDP, this represents a considerable advantage," wrote Capital Economics' \uc2 Chang Liu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Second, HKMA firmly believes changing the peg would hurt investor confidence, which could trigger catastrophic capital outflow, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2  strategist Teck Leng Tan. After all, what's the alternative?\par \par \uc2 Third, how expensive is the Hong Kong dollar really? Looking back the history, the Hong Kong dollar now is not that expensive compared to 1998, and the HKMA adamantly defended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then, argued \uc2 UBS\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 While the HKD real effective exchange rate (REER) has indeed risen to levels seen in 2009, the economic growth and inflation situation now is quite different. Also, the current level of HKD REER is still almost 40% below that seen during the 1998 Asian Financial Crisis, when HK economy was in a much more dire situation.\par \par \uc2 HKMA certainly has the means to keep the dollar peg for a while. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system means that changes in Hong Kong's monetary base are exactly matched by changes in the HKMA's US dollar holdings. Currently, HKMA has $340 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve, almost twice Hong Kong's entire monetary base of $183 billion. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can keep on buying dollars for quite a while.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, the iShares MSCI Hong Kong ETF ( EWH) fell 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 03-09-15 0645GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150903eb9300033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
