\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2  Dollar Remains Strong After  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2015\par 05:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [Dow Jones] The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is trading close to the strong side of its U.S. dollar peg, after the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected HK$15.5 billion ($2 billion) into the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to cool down the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders expect the Hong Kong dollar to remain strong as local residents continue to convert their yuan cash into the local unit as their time deposits in yuan expire. Currently, the USD/HKD hovers around 7.7501, while the HKMA is forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when either side of the 7.75-7.85 trading band is hit. The yuan's devaluation stirs debate again on whether the HKD should be pegged with it. ANZ says the USD peg is still the best regime for Hong Kong right now, given that the city's business cycle continues to follow that of the U.S. more than that of China. As long as the yuan is not fully convertible, it shouldn't be a proper anchor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the Hong Kong dollar, ANZ says. (fiona.law@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: ELM\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 02-09-15 0351GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150902eb920003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
