\par\par \b \uc2 Worried About China Slowing? Worry About Emerging Markets, Too -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2015\par 05:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] It's shaping up as a poor month for global equity markets, with China getting most of the blame--but it's dawning on markets that emerging markets which had bet on solid growth in Asia's biggest economy will suffer too. "Cracks are emerging but it's important to recognize that most of the global reaction was a belated response to slowing Chinese growth, which is not a recent phenomenon," says Alex Wolf, emerging markets economist at \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2 . "While events in China may have been the primary trigger behind last week's market upheaval, broader EM weakness is also a contributing factor," he adds. "In addition to China, institutional weakness in Brazil, Mexico, Russia and Turkey will increasingly weigh on growth prospects. As the Fed hikes, protracted EM weakness could result." The MSCI Emerging Markets Index is down 2.8% this week. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD pair has broken a declining trend line established since August 27 and remains on the upside. While it is consolidating from the high at 1.1319 marked overnight, support is provided by the rising 50-period intraday moving average. As long as 1.1230 holds as the key support, the pair is expected to challenge the first upside target at 1.1350 (last seen on August 27). However, a break below 1.1230 would call for further decline toward the first alternative downside target at 1.1200. This content was produced exclusively for Dow Jones by Trading Central ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 ), a leading investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand covered bond performance remained stable in 2Q, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Investors Services. The performance of New Zealand covered bond programs "continued to be supported by the stable Aaa rating of the New Zealand sovereign, the stable credit profiles of the issuers and the stable credit quality of the cover pool assets," it says. It was reflected in an improved average collateral score of 6.3% in 2Q compared to 6.6% in 1Q, it says. The score reflects the credit quality of the underlying mortgages. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  outlook for New Zealand's banking system is stable, reflecting our expectation that the banks will maintain their strong asset quality and stable profitability amid weaker economic growth in the coming 12-18 months," it says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ELM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  cuts China's GDP growth forecast for 2016 to 6.8% from 7.0% previously in view of slower investment growth, and sees risks tilted to the downside. "In our view, overestimating of potential growth since the mid-2000s and the excess investment are at the heart of challenges related to high levels of debt to GDP and the current disinflationary trend," it say. China's government is expected to introduce more easing policies to prevent a major slowdown. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects several more banks' reserve requirement ratio cuts in the coming quarters to keep liquidity conditions at a comfortable level and sees the possibility of one additional rate cut (25 basis points) in first-quarter next year. On the fiscal front, following the recent tax cuts for small and medium-sized enterprises, it expects more tax breaks and a faster pace of fiscal spending. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD pair is on the downside challenging the first downside target at 0.7000. It is capped by the 20-period intraday moving average, which is below the 50-period one. And the intraday relative strength index stays within the selling area between 50 and 30. A break below 0.7000 would trigger further decline toward the second downside target at 0.6950. Only a break above the key resistance at 0.7085 would turn the intraday outlook bullish and call for further bounce toward the first alternative upside target at 0.7120. This content was produced exclusively for Dow Jones by Trading Central (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ), a leading investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0303 GMT [Dow Jones] Thailand's eight consecutive months of disinflation have convinced economists that the country's inflation will likely remain low at least for the rest of this year. Official data showed the country's headline inflation fell 1.19% on-year and 0.23% on-month in August, compared with 1.04% year-over-year and 0.07% month-over-month fall in July. TISCO Economic Strategy Unit now forecasts 2015 headline inflation to contract 0.8%, widening from the 0.4% fall earlier projected. Rahul Bajoria, regional economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 , agrees that Thailand's inflation is likely to remain low for some time. "With energy prices being low, we now expect inflation to remain in negative territory through 2015, and consequently we downgrade our 2015 inflation forecast to -0.7%, from -0.4% previously," Bajoria adds. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0258 GMT [Dow Jones] Although speculation on whether the Hong Kong dollar will de-peg from the U.S. dollar and re-peg to the yuan is expected to increase after recent yuan devaluation, ANZ says the current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime is still the best system for Hong Kong right now. Contrary to common perception, Hong Kong's business cycle continues to follow that of the U.S. more closely than that of China, as the former explains 48% of Hong Kong's output while China accounts for 29% of Hong Kong output, ANZ says. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yesterday sold HK$15.5 billion twice as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit the strong end of its trading range, its first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since April. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar is fully backed under the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board arrangement and current regime can withstand market volatility, ANZ says, adding that exchange rate stability is good for a small, open, services-focused economy like Hong Kong's. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai bank exposure to small and medium enterprises--which already accounts for 39% of total loans--looks likely to become the main source of downside risks to asset quality, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2 . These loans are expected to lead to an increase in Thailand's non-performing loans during the next 12-18 months, says Fitch. It notes that the banking industry's operating environment remains "unfavorable, with relatively low economic growth, volatile financial markets and muted business sentiment." At the same time, the economy's high leverage means debt repayment ability can quickly deteriorate. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  earlier reported that the country's non-performing loans increased to 312 billion baht ($8.7 billion) in 2Q15--a 9.62% on-year rise. However, Fitch adds that Thailand's banking sector "as a whole has reasonable buffers, in term of reserves and capitalization, which should enable it to cope with a normal cyclical economic downturn." (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD Intraday: Capped by a negative trend line. After the recent pullbacks, the pair is now consolidating below its major resistance at 1.5380. A declining trend line maintains strong selling pressure on the prices. Furthermore, the 20- and 50-period intraday MAs act as resistance roles. The intraday RSI is bearish and calls for a new drop. Hence, further decline seems more likely to occur towards 1.5265 & 1.521 in extension. Alternative scenario, only the break above 1.538 would open the path to alternative upside targets at 1.5445 & 1.5500. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF Intraday: Under pressure below 0.9640. The pair remains under pressure below the key resistance at 0.9640. Both the 20- and 50-period MAs are turning down, which calls for further consolidation. And the intraday RSI indicator is mixed to bearish and below the neutrality level at 50. In these perspectives, as long as 0.9640 holds as the key resistance, look for a new decline to 0.9550 & 0.9505 in extension. Alternatively, above 0.9640 look for further upside to 0.9690 (August 31's high) at first. Trading Central (tradingcentral.com) is a commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD pair remains in a down trend and capped by a negative trend line. The intraday RSI lacks upward momentum and is below its 50 area. Besides, the bearish pattern of lower highs and lows remains intact. As long as 0.6365 (our trailing stop loss) is not surpassed, expect further weaknesses to 0.6285 & 0.6240 in extension. Alternatively, above 0.6365 look for further upside with 0.6405 & 0.643 as targets. This content was produced exclusively for Dow Jones by Trading Central (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ), a leading investment research and commentary service specializing in technical analysis. (kenneth.howe@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/JPY will likely move in a 82.70-85.50 range in choppy Asia trade with investors closely monitoring global stocks performance, says Kumiko Ishikawa, analyst at Gaitame.Com Research Institute. If Asia stocks still keep falling, the pair, now at 84.55, may fall toward its 20-day moving average of 82.62, she says. While AUD is considered a classic risk-on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , JPY is a safe haven bought in risk-off times, making the pair prone to extremely dynamic ups and down in keeping with global economic sentiment. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 01, 2015 23:18 ET (03:18 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150902eb920007q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal