\par\par \b \uc2 Lower Yuan Keeps Hong Kong Money-Changers Busy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Wei Gu And Julie Steinberg   \par \uc2 1,065 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2015\par 03:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China's devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its slowing economy and tanking stock market are weighing on Hong Kong's key industries--retailing and real estate.\par \par \uc2 But fears that things will worsen in China is a boon for the money-changers, bankers and wealth managers who are getting flooded with business from nervous Chinese eager to get their cash overseas.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong has long been the financial entry point for foreigners into China and the first place mainland Chinese visited and parked their cash. Now fears that the yuan will fall further is making the former U.K. colony, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar, a haven.\par \par \uc2 "The devaluation of the [yuan] and the volatility of the domestic market are forming a perfect storm," said \uc2 Kenny Lam\uc2 , group president of \uc2 Noah Holdings\uc2 , a Chinese wealth management firm. Mr. Lam said the company's 80-strong Hong Kong office, set up three years ago, is scrambling to meet "tremendous increase in demand" for its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated products from investors who see the yuan continuing to fall.\par \par \uc2 "Hong Kong is our clients' first stop abroad," said Mr. Lam, who worked at \uc2 McKinsey & Co\uc2 . before joining New York-listed and Shanghai-based Noah in March.\par \par \uc2 Noah's revenues rose 500% in the quarter ended June over the same period last year, the company disclosed in its recent earnings report.\par \par \uc2 The flood of cash into Hong Kong has gotten so strong the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to act on Tuesday. In its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 15.5 billion Hong Kong dollars, or $2 billion, to the market in an effort to push down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was pushing at the high end of the narrow band in which it is allowed to trade against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The pressure on the Hong Kong dollar has been building since China devalued the yuan on Aug. 11, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall 2.6% in a week. Investors had expected China to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady, and the switch sent investors fleeing the yuan.\par \par \uc2 An officer at a Chinese bank in Hong Kong said the number of people who are transferring $50,000 worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the maximum annual limit allowed a Chinese resident--from China into Hong Kong has increased several-fold in recent weeks since Beijing's devaluation of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "Generally, money moving into a city benefits a local economy because it provides more liquidity, which can fuel more investing," said Ken Peng, Asia investment strategist at Citi Private Bank in Hong Kong, adding that the bank has seen a pickup in inquiries from clients seeking to move more of their holdings into U.S. or Hong Kong dollars in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Peng said benefits for the Hong Kong economy "remain to be seen" because the impact of China's slowing economy and market selloff may make investors more reluctant to make risky investments.\par \par \uc2 Already, interest rate cuts in China and a depreciating yuan have damped a lucrative line of business for the city's bank lending into China. Chinese companies have been big borrowers from Hong Kong in recent years, taking advantage of the city's lower interest rates and a rising yuan to borrow money they then deposited in higher-earning accounts back home.\par \par \uc2 Such cross-border lending "probably was a key driver for demand the last few years" in Hong Kong for trade finance, said Jim Antos, a banking analyst at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2  Asia Ltd.--and the recent devaluation of the yuan will depress demand further still.\par \par \uc2 Banks in Hong Kong reported a 5.2% drop in loans to finance trade in July from June, the latest data from the HKMA show.\par \par \uc2 The search for havens is also keeping Hong Kong's ultra-high-end real estate homes in heavy demand, with luxury retail rents up 13% in July, over the year, according to property consultancy Colliers. A large part of that has been driven by mainland Chinese buying.\par \par \uc2 But outside high-end real estate, the outlooks for property prices and retail look grimmer. Taken together, the two sectors account for just over 10% of Hong Kong's gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Falling numbers of Chinese tourists, and China's anticorruption crackdown, are hurting spending in the city. The number of visitors to Hong Kong fell 8.4% in July, from a growth of 11.2% a year earlier, according to data from the \uc2 Hong Kong Tourism Board\uc2  released Monday. Most of that was from an almost 10% drop in mainland Chinese tourists that month. Even before the yuan's devaluation, spending by Chinese tourists in Hong Kong had been falling, with retail sales down for its fifth straight month by 2.8% in July.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, New York-based handbag maker Coach shut its flagship store on Queen's Road Central, a road in the heart of Hong Kong's business district and home to some of the world's highest rents. Coach is backing out of a lease set to expire in 2017, according to retail leasing brokers. Coach declined to comment on its lease.\par \par \uc2 "The retail network is saturated for many brands in Hong Kong," said Karim Azar, general manager of retail leasing at International Financial Center, a luxury shopping mall.\par \par \uc2 Real estate sales volumes fell 30% in Hong Kong in July over the previous year, according to the Land Registry, showing that the weakening Hong Kong economy, which is increasingly linked to the economic well-being of China, is hurting confidence and sentiment.\par \par \uc2 For now, however, analysts say the biggest risk to Hong Kong lies in higher U.S. interest rates, which the city would have to mirror because of the peg.\par \par \uc2 "The real risk for the [Hong Kong] economy comes from [Hong Kong] interest rates rising alongside U.S. interest rates at a time when [Hong Kong] faces a peaking credit cycle and property market, reduced China tourist spending, weakening exports and a China economic slowdown," \uc2 UBS Group AG\uc2  said in a note Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Fiona Law contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to \uc2 Wei Gu\uc2  at wei.gu@wsj.com and Julie Steinberg at julie.steinberg@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 02-09-15 0132GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020150902eb920001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
