\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia's KLCI Starts Sept Lower as Regional Stocks Down -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2015\par 11:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0954 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] A regional stock selloff led by China sucks in Malaysian stocks with it, Tuesday. The FTSE Bursa Malaysia KLCI ends 0.2% lower at 1609.21, after manufacturing data from the big northern neighbor disappoints. \uc2 CIMB Group Holdings\uc2  (1023.KU) leads large-cap declines, down 3.2%, while DiGi.com (6947.KU) loses 2.9% and \uc2 Kuala Lumpur Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) falls 2%. SapuraKencana Petroleum (5218.KU) gains 4.1% despite falling oil prices, while Genting (3182.KU) leads gainers, ending 4.2% higher. In local news, Malaysia reported its own manufacturing activity reading for August, which fell as orders contracted at the fastest rate since September 2012, dragging further on stocks. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0949 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Singapore's ruling People's Action Party will face its broadest challenge since Singapore gained independence 50 years ago, as the opposition parties will contest all 89 parliamentary seats in the September 11 elections. This surpasses the previous record in 2011, when the opposition contested 82 out of 87 seats. In 2006, only 47 out of 84 seats were contested. Prime Minister \uc2 Lee Hsien Loong\uc2  will be hoping to reverse PAP's setback in the 2011 elections, when it received its lowest-ever share of the popular vote at 60.1%, though it held on to 81 of the parliament's then-87 seats. The government has since attempted to tackle voter discontent over issues such as rising housing and transportation costs, widening income inequality and an influx of foreign labor. The election will test whether measures such as tightening work-visa requirements--which have been welcomed by Singaporeans--will arrest the government's slide in popularity. No one expects the opposition to win a parliamentary majority or to seriously challenge the PAP's hold on power. (venkat.pr@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0947 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/PHP is lower Tuesday, tracking the greenback's move against other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair closed at 46.685 to the US dollar after settling Friday at 46.735. A local bank trader said US dollar remittance inflows during the extended weekend also aided the Philippine peso Tuesday. Manila markets were closed Monday for a holiday. Volume is moderate. Traders expect the USD/PHP to continue moving within the 46.50-47.00 range, with an impending hike in US interest rates likely to underpin support for the greenback.(cris.larano@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0943 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] State-owned \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vietnam Oil & Gas\f28 \uc2 \f2  Group, or \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroVietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said it will be facing numerous difficulties over the next several years. \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroVietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it will be difficult to raise funds for its projects due to the slowness of the global economic recovery, weaker prices for crude oil and other non-market factors. It said it will be more expensive to explore for and produce oil, adding that the company still lacks experienced workers. \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroVietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2  is the country's largest company, contributing a quarter of Vietnam's budget revenue and around 10% of its gross domestic product. (trong-khanh.vu@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @TrongKhanhVu) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0942 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Inflation in the Philippines likely decelerated further in August to its lowest in over two decades, slowed by steady decline in fuel prices. The median forecast of 11 economists in a Wall Street Journal poll predict that the increase in consumer price index, the country's main inflation barometer, in August was at 0.6% from the year-earlier period, slower than the 0.8% inflation registered in July. "Oil companies announced pull backs in retail prices as global oil prices declined to new six-year lows. Food price gains remain subdued, while the expected higher prices in electricity did not pan out," said Eugenia Victorino, an economist at ANZ. The still-benign inflation outlook will allow the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas\f28 \uc2 \f2  to keep policy rates steady at its meeting later in September even if the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  raises interest rates. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forecasts August inflation to settle within the 0.2%-1.0% range. August data is due 0100 GMT, Sept. 4. (cris.larano@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0937 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Singapore companies are positive about their business and trade outlook for the next six months, with client acquisition and international expansion being key drivers of growth, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's latest trade confidence survey. The survey captured the views of 132 businesses in Singapore with an annual turnover of US$10 million and up. According to the report, 79% of the companies say they aimed to grow by acquiring new customers, while close to half anticipate an increase to their cross-broader trade volume. Only 16% say they expect cross-border trade to fall. (venkat.pr@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0928 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees a 50/50 probability that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Riksbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  will cut rates Thursday, but at the very least it will be dovish, likely lowering inflation forecasts. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 The ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  will also be dovish on Thursday but we believe that the risks are skewed towards the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Riksbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  being more dovish than \f28 \uc2 \f2 the ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  and hence EUR/SEK squeezing slightly higher." EUR/SEK trades at 9.5185. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0926 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The strengthening in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar, and subsequently the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is a result of local residents converting the yuan back to the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders say. The HKMA on Tuesday \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and sold HK$6.2 billion (US$800 million) as the Hong Kong dollar hit the limit of its U.S. dollar peg. Over the last decade, Hong Kong residents have been accumulating lots of yuan on the view that it was on a one-way route to appreciation. Now, worries mount as Beijing last month devalued the yuan and allowed bigger market forces to determine the yuan's value. "As some residents have their RMB time deposits expired recently, they're converting the money back to Hong Kong dollar amid fear of long-term depreciation of the RMB," a local trader at a Singapore bank says. With the yuan deposits in Hong Kong totaling 994.1 billion yuan at end-July, the wave of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conversion and the support to the Hong Kong dollar can hardly be neglected. The devaluation's impact on the city's yuan deposits will be reflected in August data scheduled to release by the HKMA. (fiona.law@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0916 GMT In a welcome development Italian consumers have helped the country's final 2Q GDP expansion, topping estimates. National statistical office Istat says 2Q GDP rises 0.3% on the quarter in final data, while it adds 0.7% on the year. Istat preliminary data had a 0.2% gain on the quarter and a 0.5% increase on the year. Economists polled by The Wall Street Journal had predicted monthly and yearly expansion to match preliminary data. Italian households contributed 0.3 percentage points to the quarterly growth, with acquisitions of durable goods particularly strong. (liam.moloney@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0912 GMT The eurozone's unemployment rate fell to 10.9% in July from 11.1% in June, thanks to large drops in the numbers of people without jobs in both Italy and Spain. That will encourage policy makers to believe reforms to labor markets over recent years are bearing fruit, and for \f28 \uc2 \f2 the ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  in particular will suggest inflationary pressures will build as the output gap starts to narrow. Economists had expected the jobless rate to be unchanged, and this is the first positive surprise from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area for several months. (paul.hannon@wsj.com, @PaulHannon29) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0905 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities slump in the afternoon session, underperforming the Shanghai Composite after disappointing PMI data from China and warnings from investment banks on the strength of the city's economic growth. "We have maintained a cautious view on Hong Kong's retail sales, external trade and overall growth momentum in the coming quarters, under the dual pressure from a weak Chinese economy and risk of higher US interest rates going forward," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Stress in asset market performance is sensitive to the sentiments of economic agents, likely to cause greater-than-expected headwinds in overall growth." The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index falls 2.2% to 21,185.43, while H-shares tumble, underperforming the Shanghai Composite. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index falls 3% to 9,454.11. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0854 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The recent corporate governance reform will likely prompt Japanese industrial corporations to consider M&A as an important option to improve profitability, U.S. credit rating agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a report Tuesday. "Although acquisition costs could easily surge because of the weak yen and a robust global M&A market in recent years, we believe Japanese corporations are likely to take a positive stance toward overseas M&A in their development of medium- to long-term strategies," the report says. According to Dealogic, Japanese cross-border M&A volume reached $69.5 billion this year through August 14, up from the $43.5 billion announced in the same period a year ago and a record level for the period. (atsuko.fukase@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 01, 2015 05:54 ET (09:54 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150901eb910011u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw20