\par\par \b \uc2 China Woes Drive Hong Kong, Taiwan to Bear Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2015\par 14:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Stocks in Hong Kong and Taiwan fell into bear-market territory Thursday, as the effects of China's volatility spills across its borders.\par \par \uc2 Both Hong Kong's \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index and Taiwan's Taiex fell 20% from their peaks in April earlier Thursday before retracing losses. The \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index closed down 1.8% and Taiwan's Taiex, which was down as much as 0.9%, rebounded to end 0.1% higher.\par \par \uc2 The accessibility of Hong Kong's market's to foreigners--in contrast to China's markets--make it a proxy for investing in China. That easier access makes Hong Kong vulnerable to losses amid mounting worries about the health of China's markets and economy. About half of shares listed on the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  are so-called H-shares, which represent Chinese companies listed in the city.\par \par \uc2 For example, when mainland stocks were suspended from trading in the throes of China's stock turmoil--with as few as 3% of stocks available for trading at one point--some investors who wanted to pull money from the Chinese market had to sell their stocks in Hong Kong instead, said Binay Chandgothia, a portfolio manager at \uc2 Principal Global Investors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the streak of volatility continues to rattle investors in China's markets, with heavy selling in the last 15 minutes of trading Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index ended down 3.4% at 3664.29 and the smaller Shenzhen Composite fell 3% to 2155.49.\par \par \uc2 Global investors withdrew 1.34 billion yuan ($209.45 million) from Shanghai via a trading link with \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty about the pace of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Shanghai and Shenzhen has spurred much of this week's rocky trading, as the funds tasked with supporting the market don't disclose details of what and when they are buying. Earlier this week, some listed firms announced government-held stakes, which lifted shares, though performance across the market has been uneven.\par \par \uc2 Those that disclosed Wednesday after market hours that the state fund Central Huijin Investment has increased its stake, for example, contained their losses Thursday: \uc2 Agricultural Bank of China Ltd\uc2 . fell 0.3%, \uc2 Bank of China Ltd\uc2 . slipped 0.5% and \uc2 Industrial and Commercial Bank of China Ltd\uc2 . declined 0.4%. Losses in other big state-owned companies were steeper. For example, \uc2 PetroChina Co\uc2 . sank 4.4%, while \uc2 China Citic Bank Corp\uc2 . shed 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, worries about the timing of a rise in U.S. interest rates, which could crimp growth and profits, drove losses, with Australia's \uc2 S&P\uc2 ASX\uc2  200 down 1.7%, Nikkei's Stock Average down 0.9% and South Korea's Kospi down 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, minutes from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy meeting provided no clear sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise rates as soon as September.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are in a state of heightened nervousness so comments about global uncertainty get latched on to," including the Fed's minutes, said Shane Oliver, an investment strategist at AMP Capital.\par \par \uc2 A rush to safer assets drove gold prices to a near five week-high in Asia trade. Spot gold rose to an intraday high of $1,141.80 before cooling to $1,137.90.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan weakened in the morning, after the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  confirmed an earlier proposal to a delay in the five-year reshuffling of the basket, which currently doesn't include the yuan. Late Wednesday, the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's executive board approved an extension of the current basket of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  included in its special drawing rights to Sept. 30, 2016.\par \par \uc2 AMP's Mr. Oliver said that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  decision added to jitters among Asian investors even though a selloff in the region was already under way after China devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 The yuan recovered to finish roughly flat onshore. Offshore, it was last 0.2% stronger at 6.4451 against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude oil was down 1.4% at $46.51 a barrel in Asia trade, amid worries about future demand from China.\par \par \uc2 Biman Mukherji contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gregor Stuart Hunter at gregor.hunter@wsj.com and Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 20, 2015 08:10 ET (12:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150820eb8k001ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
