\par\par \b \uc2 China Meltdown 'Painful' but Necessary Lesson for Regulators -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2015\par 06:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Chinese markets' recent meltdown will offer a "painful lesson" for regulators who trumpeted the rally, only to scramble to arrest the selloff of the past four weeks, says Francis Cheung, head of China-HK Strategy at brokerage CLSA. Before the market correction, financial reforms such as insuring deposits and reforming bloated state-owned enterprises were "in the right direction" before the market began to tank in mid-June, says Cheung. So why couldn't policymakers see a market downturn was coming? While some regulators did recognize the dangerous level of leverage in the market, none wanted to stick his head out to question it, says Cheung. Add in a lack of coordination among various securities and market regulators, and the market run was allowed to continue.\par \par \uc2  While CLSA declined to speculate on government steps going forward, Cheung said that liberalization will likely continue, and that the market correction is a "good learning lesson for regulators." (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0420 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The real data point to watch on the Chinese foreign trade front is imports, not the exports that tend to get most of the attention, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Huatai Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lu Ting\f28 \uc2 \f2  in an interview. Imports in June fell 6.1% year on year in dollar terms, compared with a 17.6% decline in May, while exports rose 2.8% last month compared with a 2.5% decline in May. "If you see rising imports, then that perhaps signals that the Chinese economy is rebounding -- imports are more important than exports in reflecting the health of the Chinese economy," Mr. Lu says. "Overall, the economy is still weak. I don't see a clear rebound". (Mark.Magnier@WSJ.com; Twitter @MarkMagnier)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0422 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Singapore Telecommunications\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SGAPY) unit Optus is continuing its aggressive bid for market share in the Australian mobile market but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TLS.AU) investors need not rush for the exits, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The broker says Optus has overtaken \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vodafone\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VOD.LN) as best value in the market by again adding data value and voice minutes to its pre paid plans in July. Optus holds about 37% prepaid market share, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2  at 45%, and "with Optus now overtaking \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vodafone\f28 \uc2 \f2  as the most competitive provider we see potential share movement from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2 ". However, the Australian mobile base is mainly post paid with pre-paid accounting for only 38% of the market, "hence the earnings impact of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2  losing here is minimal". Keeps Equal Weight on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0414 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  will likely keep the benchmark BI rate unchanged at 7.50% Tuesday, as lingering uncertainties in the global market keep the rupiah vulnerable. Even though the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been relatively stable recently during the Greek crisis, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  is unlikely to risk making the rupiah more susceptible to capital outflow. "Volatility in the financial markets, and especially the weak sentiment on the rupiah, means there is not much that BI can do at this juncture. Focusing on financial system stability is the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's new mantra," says DBS economist Gundy Cahyadi. All of the 10 economists polled by the Wall Street Journal also expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stay at the current rate because of lingering concerns over high inflation and offshore financing risks. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ELM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0420 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Offshore events continue to be the key drivers for the New Zealand dollar with events in China the most capable of driving a "sudden" change in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week, says ANZ in a note. It adds that while the stabilization in the Chinese stock market eases some of the risks facing the kiwi, June trade data and second quarter GDP ensures that China "remains at the forefront for NZD valuation." ANZ adds that U.S. Fed Chair Yellen's testimony to Congress and upcoming release of July activity data is expected to continue to keep the NZD/USD pair under pressure, as she is likely to continue to signal rate rises are likely in 2015. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; Twitter: @lucy_craymer) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0410 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 University of Michigan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Stephen M. Ross School of Business said it has received a pledge of $60 million for its entrepreneurship center from the Zell Family Foundation. A $10 million portion of the gift will be set aside specifically to invest in student ventures, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alison Davis-Blake\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the school's dean, while the remainder will be directed toward initiatives like elective courses and extracurricular experiences such as trips. Students will be involved in vetting their classmates' companies as they compete for funding, Davis-Blake said. The gift comes as many top business schools continue to pump resources into entrepreneurship programs. (Lindsay.gellman@wsj.com; Twitter: @lindsaygellman) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0406 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The latest U.S. forecast from the National Oceanic and Atmospeheric Administration that El Nino could last through until Spring 2016 is worrying "because the longer it (El Nino) lasts the more opportunity there is for crop damage because some of the crops have a longer planting cycle," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Joseph Incalcaterra. He adds that furthermore crops like palm oil are more resilient to drought conditions but if it drags on they too could be impacted by the weather pattern. He notes that the Indian monsoon is currently tracking near average levels but July remains key to whether the monsoon season delivers sufficient rainfall to the country. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;Twitter: @lucy_craymer) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ALP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0404 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The exit of another director at Sound Global (0967.HK) has intensified scrutiny of the firm's corporate governance. This as it tries to account for a 2 billion yuan cash shortfall identified by an auditor, and highlighted in a February report by short-seller Emerson Analytics. An independent director, Fu Tao, resigned after market close Friday--citing "no disagreement" with the board--and his replacement has ties to a company planning to buy a 30% stake in Sound Global. That raises questions about whether the new director is really independent at all, says analyst Trung Nguyen of Lucror Analytics. Three months prior, Wong See Meng, an independent director and former head of Sound Global's audit committee, left the firm. The leadership switch follows an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Stock Exchange, which has ordered Sound Global to have an exchange-approved "forensic specialist" investigate the cash shortfall, among other required conditions, before shares can resume trading again. (Dominique.fong@wsj.com; Twitter: @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0346 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Data is expected to show that New Zealand inflation picked up in the second quarter but remains well below the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range of 1% to 3%, according to a Wall Street Journal poll of 14 economists. The median from the poll sees the consumer-price index rising 0.6% vs. 1Q, while it is expected to be up 0.4% on year. This compares with a fall of 0.3% on quarter in 1Q and annual inflation sitting at just 0.1% on year. The data is due to be released at 2245 GMT on Wednesday. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;Twitter: @lucy_craymer) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: AAP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0336 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's trade data points to softer growth in the second-quarter, says ANZ economist Liu Ligang in a note. In USD terms, China's first-half exports rose about 1% year-over-year while imports fell 15.5% from a year earlier. "Both exports and imports were weak," writes Liu, and forecasts GDP growth to fall to 6.8% in the second-quarter. Growth in the world's second-largest economy grew 7% in the first-half, the lowest in decades. China is set to release 2Q GDP Tuesday morning. A poll of 14 economists by the Wall Street Journal estimates growth for the quarter at 6.8%. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0344 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pioneer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global (0224.HK) jumps as much as 29% Monday after the Hong Kong-listed property and hotel investor joined a consortium to buy a luxury hotel in Hong Kong from \f28 \uc2 \f2 InterContinental Hotels Group PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (IHG.LN). Gains of the stock narrow to 8.6% at HK$1.52, still outperforming the broader market's 0.4% decline. \f28 \uc2 \f2 InterContinental Hotels Group PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (IHG.LN). on Friday sealed a US$938-million deal with a group of investors including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pioneer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global and others advised by real-estate private equity firm Gaw Capital Partners, making the single biggest sale of a hotel property in terms of transaction value in the city's history. InterContinental Hong Kong will be \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pioneer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global's first hotel project in Hong Kong alongside its existing hotel investments in Bangkok, Pattaya and Yangon. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com, Twitter: @joannechiuhk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0338 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hotel Shilla\f28 \uc2 \f2  (008770.SE) rise 3.1% to 132,000 won, outperforming the broader South Korean market's 0.8% gain, after it won a license on Friday to operate a new duty-free store in Seoul. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Hotel Shilla\f28 \uc2 \f2  looks set to enjoy a substantial earnings increase in 2016 due to the new license and favorable product mix change," says Nomura analyst Cara Song. She chooses \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hotel Shilla\f28 \uc2 \f2  as her top pick in Korea's consumer sector, discarding her earlier choice, Amorepacific (090430.SE). She also raises her target price for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hotel Shilla\f28 \uc2 \f2  by 80% to 230,000 won with a buy recommendation. (in-soo.nam@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DWT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 13, 2015 00:31 ET (04:31 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150713eb7d000bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
