\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Down 0.1%; Downward Outlook Would Be No Surprise -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2015\par 06:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei is down 0.1% at 19993.34, little moved from early trading hours. Volume remains tepid at under 1.0 billion shares. Chances are reasonably good that, given signs of a weak consumption rebound and maturing economic expansion, upcoming government assessments of the economy will be slightly downgraded, says Hajime Kitano, Japan equity strategist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . "Thus it would not be surprising if the outlook back in January (when the consensus was 15% earnings growth and an end-2015 Nikkei Average of 20,000) were to be revised downward, although share prices are actually ahead of pace based on that January forecast," he says. It is within this context that market participants are greeting the rising market with a mix of belief and skepticism." If a stock market downturn is indeed coming, low-beta (defensive) sectors while overvalued, will nevertheless outperform, he adds. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Fund managers are still underweighting Chinese stocks but the recent rally may force them to raise their exposure, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Only 20% of emerging markets funds and 40% of Asia ex-Japan funds have outperformed their benchmarks so far this year, the worst performance since 2009, as the rally in Hong Kong took the fund managers by surprise, according to Goldman. "The rally in A shares doesn't hurt since it is out of benchmark," it says. "But the China offshore market is within benchmark for most managers, so underexposure has directly led to underperformance." It estimates mutual funds have underweighted China by 140-600bps versus their benchmarks, with financials, telcos and energy the most underweighted sectors. It says the potential inflows could be significant if the funds decided to bring their allocations in Chinese stocks to benchmark levels. (jacky.wong@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @jackycwong) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0413 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong equities advance in the morning session as large cap stocks drive gains. Sinopec (0386.HK) rises 5.8% to HK$7.53 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroChina Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (0857.HK) is up 4.9% at HK$10.46, leading gains as a gauge of Hong Kong-listed energy stocks jumps 3.6%. Amid mainland media reports that state-owned enterprises may be consolidated, "reform euphoria" is driving investor confidence today, says Reorient Group, with shipping companies and oil stocks outperforming. \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0005.HK) is up 5.1% to HK$77.60 after a report in the Sunday Times that it is reviewing whether to sell its UK retail banking business amid talks over the future of its headquarters. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index gains 1.3% to 28,426.72, while H-shares lag a 1.9% gain in the Shanghai Composite; the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hang Seng\f28 \uc2 \f2  China Enterprises Index advances 0.9% to 14,619.91. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @gregorhunter) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0409 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Former Australian conservative leader Malcolm Turnbull is now clearly preferred as Liberal Party Leader by 38% of electors, up 2% since January 12-13, 2015, and well ahead of Deputy Leader Julie Bishop 27% (up 1%), and Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tony Abbott\f28 \uc2 \f2  on 12% (down 2%), according to the latest Morgan Poll. This is a record low level of support for Abbott since he became Liberal leader in December 2009. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0359 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Cash keeps flooding into Hong Kong markets, reflected by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's continuous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar hit the strong side of its trading band multiple times over the weekend. The de facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold a combined 16.8 billion Hong Kong dollars ($2.2 billion) to cool the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Since early April it has injected HK$71.5 billion ($9.2 billion) to defend the dollar peg as the Hong Kong stock market heated up with a strong appetite from mainland Chinese investors. The cash injection is close to the HK$75.3 billion poured in during its previous major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in July-August 2014. In Asia's Monday morning session, the USD/HKD still hovers around 7.7502, close to the lower end of the 7.75-7.85 range in which the pair is allowed to trade, while the local stocks have risen 1.4%. (fiona.law@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0344 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/INR, which hit a 4-month high of 63.67 on Friday, may initially retreat due to the U.S. dollar index falling again on weaker-than-expected U.S. durable goods orders data. But the bullish technical bias of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel will remain--suggesting the rupee keeps weakening versus the greenback--as long as USD/INR ends the day above 62.93. While most analysts expect recently weak U.S. economic data to influence the U.S. Federal Reserve to maintain a dovish stance at its Wednesday monetary policy meeting, the even-more dovish stance of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 India \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  still makes it attractive to be long USD/INR on the basis of yield-differential. The rupee has been under pressure since the Reserve Bank of India governor last week said additional monetary policy easing is an option as inflation has slowed. USD/INR closed Friday at 63.65. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0341 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Increased spending by the South Korean government could be an effective policy option later this year to prevent a recurrence of soft economic growth that was seen last year, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hana Daetoo Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Kim Doo-un. "The government should keep the door open for working out an additional national budget in the second half of this year [so as to] not to repeat what happened in the final quarter of last year," Kim says. Unexpected shortfalls in tax revenue and thus reduced government spending led Korea's economy to grow at a mere 0.3% rate (on-quarter) in the final quarter of 2014. The economy rebounded moderately to grow 0.8% in the first quarter of this year. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  last lowered its base rate to a record low of 1.75% in March. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0327 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] International retailers already have a large footprint in Southeast Asia's megacities like Bangkok, Jakarta and Manila, but market research firm Nielsen said Monday that the big opportunity to reach new consumers over the coming decade will emerge in the region's smaller cities. By 2025, two-thirds of Asean's urban population will live in cities of 500,000 people or less, Nieslen said, with cities of this size expected to account for most of the region's urban population growth. Over 230 million people will live in the region's smaller cities a decade from now, according to Nielsen's forecast, meaning that retailers seeking growth should seek out "new consumption hotspots" where they can reach fast-growing, underserved markets in Southeast Asia's lesser-known cities. (trefor.moss@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0308 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] It's not always that the Singapore dollar reacts to local economic data, given the externally oriented nature of the tiny nation's economy. But as the local unit continues to strengthen against the U.S. dollar, traders have been taking cues from data reports this month. The USD/SGD is now at 1.3325, bouncing off a three-month low of 1.3304 on Friday after Singapore said industrial output fell 5.5% on year. The outcome was slightly better than the 6.1% fall predicted by a Dow Jones Newswires poll. "This is the third number in a series of stronger-than-expected data released in the last two weeks which has led to a sell-down of the Dollar-Sing from as high at 1.3750 in the middle of April," CMC Markets analyst Nicholas Teo says. Fresh cues will likely come from U.S. data and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announcement this week, he says. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/IDR is a tad higher Monday but may soon slide into the daily Bollinger downtrend channel as the greenback declines broadly in Asia on the tailwind of Friday's drop in \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields. If USD/IDR ends Monday below 12,890 it would be inside the Bollinger downtrend channel and could then be on its way to 12,540 where the weekly downtrend channel awaits. The yield on the benchmark U.S. 10-year Treasury note fell to 1.91% on Friday from around 1.96% after U.S. durable goods orders fell below expectations. A lower yield on \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds makes it less appealing to hold the dollar and makes high-yielding, if riskier, emerging market bonds such as those of Indonesia more attractive. USD/IDR is now 12,925 from its Friday close of 12,915. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0231 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY will likely trade in a 118-120 range over the short term, with a slew of orders densely placed on both the upside and downside trapping the pair in a tight range, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Marito Ueda\f28 \uc2 \f2 , director at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Prime byGMO. Mr. Ueda says there have been orders lining up for the USD selling from 119.50 and above, while USD buying orders are concentrated from Y118.50 and below. "As far as we can tell from the orders, the market needs considerable energy to step out of either side" of the current range, says Mr. Ueda. That energy could come from surprises from the FOMC or the BOJ, as well as from comments by Chairwoman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  or BOJ Gov. Haruhiko Kuroda.(hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor SXS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0222 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Some argue that the Australian government can currently borrow at historically low interest rates, adding that concern about Australia's fiscal position is misplaced. Adam Boyton, chief economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  begs to differ. "We see at least three problems with such complacency," he said. First, the starting point for the budget deficit heading into a period of demographic change matters enormously; second, Australia runs persistent current account deficits that can make the economy vulnerable to swings in offshore investor sentiment. Reflecting this, a strong fiscal position has been an important counter-weight to Australia's current account deficit and large external liabilities since the late 1980s; third, large ongoing deficits will weaken Australia's ability to respond to external shocks. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 27, 2015 00:20 ET (04:20 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150427eb4r000eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
