\par\par \b \uc2 China stocks hit 7-yr high despite weak factory activity; Hong Kong also up\b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2015\par 06:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * CSI300 +0.2 pct; SSEC: 0.2 pct; HSI: 0.6 pct               \par \par \uc2 * China April flash \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI contracts to one-year low               \par \par \uc2 * Net flow into China's stock market totalled 781.4 billion yuan last week               \par \par \uc2 By Samuel Shen and Pete Sweeney               \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, April 23 (Reuters) - China stocks advanced to fresh seven-year highs on Thursday as weaker-than-expected factory activity data reinforced expectations that Beijing will roll out more stimulus measures and keep the financial system flush with cash.               \par \par \uc2 The flash \uc2 HSBC\uc2 /Markit Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) showed that China's factory activity in April contracted at its fastest pace in a year, suggesting that economic conditions are still deteriorating.               \par \par \uc2 Investors also shrugged off the prospects of weaker company earnings. A Reuters survey showed that earnings growth at China-listed companies is likely to be the slowest in three years in 2015.               \par \par \uc2 "No one cares about price/earnings ratios, or price/book ratios now. Investors only care about the attitude of the government, which has so far appeared tolerant (of the rise)," said Du Changchun, analyst at Northeast Securities in Shanghai.               \par \par \uc2 "Upward momentum is still very strong, as money keeps flooding in. I don't dare to forecast the market's peak."               \par \par \uc2 The CSI300 index rose 0.2 percent to 4,751.24 points by midday, while the Shanghai Composite Index  gained 0.2 percent to 4,407.72 points.               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  index added 0.6 percent to 28,091.63 points, while the Hong Kong China Enterprises Index  gained 0.4 percent to 14,722.25.               \par \par \uc2 Despite the market's 80 percent surge since last November, new investors keep piling in. Last week alone, there was a net flow of 781.4 billion yuan ($126.08 billion) into the stock market, marking the fourth consecutive week of net inflows.               \par \par \uc2 And new stock trading accounts hit a record 3.3 million last week, almost doubling from the previous week, although that also  reflected a rule change that let investors open multiple share accounts.               \par \par \uc2 In the words of \uc2 UBS\uc2 ' Asian strategists, the action seen in Chinese stocks is what happens "when 'Animal Spirits' meet liquidity."               \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA), \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again, selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the trading band against the U.S. dollar, reflecting strong money inflows.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese steelmakers rose sharply as investors' interest shifts to some relatively cheaper cyclical stocks. Internet-related stocks were also strong.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-4-23T05:44:42.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150423eb4n005ej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
