\par\par \b \uc2 Update: \uc2 Hong Kong  \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority \highlight\uc2  Expects More Stock Volatility From Cash Inflows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Fiona Law   \par \uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2015\par 14:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--The head of Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said he expects increasing volatility in the city's stock market as cash floods in from the mainland and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Heavy fund flows have forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  five times in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since last Thursday to prevent the Hong Kong dollar from strengthening past the upper limit of its narrow trading band.\par \par \uc2 Since the U.S. Federal Reserve started its "quantitative easing" bond-buying program to stimulate its economy in late 2008, funds have flocked to Hong Kong in hopes of big returns--pushing stocks and property prices sharply higher and driving up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The buying has picked up since China recently opened the gate wider for mainland investors to buy Hong Kong stocks. Mainland cash helped send Hong Kong shares up 7.9% last week.\par \par \uc2 The launch last year of the Shanghai-Hong Kong Stock Connect program--allowing investors in Shanghai to trade eligible shares in Hong Kong, and vice versa--and a planned connection between the Shenzhen and Hong Kong markets, means that "mainland investors' appetite and decisions will affect the Hong Kong stock market," chief executive of the authority Norman Chan said Wednesday. "Cash inflows and outflows from Hong Kong will become more frequent, and thus there will be more ups and downs in the local markets."\par \par \uc2 The inflows have forced to HKMA to inject 33.79 billion Hong Kong dollars (US$4.36 billion) to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Thursday. The HKMA is obliged to sell the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it touches the high side of the authority's HK$7.75-HK$7.85 band against the U.S. dollar, and to buy when it touches the low side.\par \par \uc2 The HKMA was last forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July and August, when it sold HK$75.3 billion in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Hong Kong stocks went up about 7%.\par \par \uc2 Since 2008, the \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  Index has almost doubled. Local housing prices, meanwhile, have more than doubled, according to the Centa-City Leading Index, which is compiled by Centaline Property Agency.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether the fresh round of fund inflows would further fuel the property market, Mr. Chan said he wouldn't rule it out, as there is no clear tracker on where the cash would go.\par \par \uc2 "The flow of money into HK (would take) time to dissipate through the asset markets, supporting stocks and in turn property," said Patrick Bennett, macro strategy analyst at CIBC. "However, in the current environment where the flow of money to and from the mainland is significantly more open, any early signs of profit-taking may make the elevated prices be somewhat shorter."\par \par \uc2 In recent years the HKMA has rolled out a series of measures to cool down the red-hot property market, including more restrictive mortgage and tax policies.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar peg is widely seen as the culprit for inflation in recent years, including higher property prices, raising calls for a peg to the Chinese yuan instead, given Hong Kong's growing links with the mainland economy and the yuan's increasingly liberalized status. But the authorities appear to be sticking with the status quo.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system has been operating well," said Mr. Chan.\par \par \uc2 Write to Fiona Law at fiona.law@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 15, 2015 08:15 ET (12:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150415eb4f001pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
