\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar surges anew, euro zone data eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 13:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Dollar hits new highs against yen, euro, overall \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket\par \par \uc2 * Kiwi at lowest in over a year as RBNZ data confirm \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar falters after pro-democracy demonstrations\par \par \uc2 * CFTC data show speculators increased dollar bets\par \par \uc2 By \uc2 Patrick Graham\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 29 (Reuters) - The dollar traded just off multi-year highs against the yen, euro and a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, as a three-month-old rally showed no signs of dissipating before a week of important economic set pieces.\par \par \uc2 The week's big release in the United States is non-farm payrolls on Friday, but a raft of other data comes before that. They should drive home the message sent by gross domestic product numbers last week of an increasingly robust recovery.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, euro zone data showed economic sentiment deteriorated in September to levels last seen in late 2013, underlining a divergence in fortunes that has driven the euro more than 9 percent lower since May.\par \par \uc2 The biggest mover among developed-world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the New Zealand dollar. Figures showed the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August.\par \par \uc2 That, and weekend protests in Hong Kong, added to the sense of a more turbulent period ahead for markets as the U.S. Federal Reserve brings an end to a programme of bond-buying that has pumped trillions of dollars into the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "I do wonder if there's a bit of risk aversion sliding into the dollar rally now," said Adam Myers, European head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "If we do see a bit of a back-up in performance in emerging markets, then we may see people moving towards buying dollars as a safe haven on top of the strength we have seen."\par \par \uc2 The kiwi was some 1.3 percent lower at $0.7768. The yen was another quarter point lower at 109.55, just off a new six-year high for the dollar of 109.75 yen.\par \par \uc2 Asian market attention turned to Hong Kong, where democracy protesters defied volleys of tear gas and police baton charges in the financial centre.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the Hong Kong dollar was stable after falling to a six-month low. Pegged to the U.S. dollar, it was last down about 0.1 percent at 7.7649.\par \par \uc2 INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Data released by the \uc2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2  on Friday showed speculators increased their bullish bets on the U.S. dollar in the week ending Sept. 23. The value of the dollar's net long position rose to $35.81 billion from $31.42 billion the previous week.\par \par \uc2 "There is definitely the feeling that the dollar is moving towards overbought territory," Myers said. "If we get a weak payrolls number at the end of the week, we may well see a decent correction next week."\par \par \uc2 Economists expect the payrolls report will show U.S. employers hired 219,000 people in September, a rebound from August's rise of only 142,000.\par \par \uc2 The essence of the dollar's rise since July has been the divergence of the U.S. economy's direction from those of Europe and Japan. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  is beginning to rein in years of loose monetary policy; the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are under pressure to do more for growth.\par \par \uc2 One question is whether the \uc2 Fed\uc2  will be as happy tightening interest rates next year if the dollar continues to gain so quickly. Some banks predict a fall for the euro to parity or close to parity with the dollar over the next couple of years,\par \par \uc2 "I don't think it's too strong to call what we have now \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars," said Simon Derrick, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Bank of New York Mellon\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. was very effective after 2008 in talking down the dollar and (ECB chief) \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  has done exactly that in the past few months. The question is will the \uc2 Fed\uc2  be happy about it if the euro keeps falling." (Editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-29T12:16:05.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140929ea9t009mz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
