\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan extends rise past midpoint on bullish bets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee   \par \uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2014\par 05:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Aug 7 (Reuters) - China's yuan rose past the midpoint for a second day on Thursday, as investors ramped up bets the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will notch up strong gains in the coming months due to a stabilising economy.\par \par \uc2 Investors have generally turned bullish on the Chinese economy sending the stock market up 8 percent in roughly three weeks and the yuan stronger, in spite of a weak property market and recently patchy economic data.\par \par \uc2 In late morning trade on Thursday, the yuan was changing hands at 6.1580 per dollar, stronger than a daily midpoint fixing of 6.1670 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise past the midpoint on Wednesday was the first time since September 2012 the spot crossed the midpoint to the strong side of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's guidance rate that set off a long-running rally that lasted into early 2014.\par \par \uc2 Investors are betting the latest rally may have strong legs.\par \par \uc2 With the watchword being stability, Beijing would be loathe to tolerate any large capital outflows from the onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the wake of any significant shift in the U.S. Federal Reserve in the coming months and instead may let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gain as it did in 2012, strategists at Gavekal Research said.\par \par \uc2 "Consequently, further near-term depreciation looks unlikely," they wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 Capital flows have become bigger and even more lumpy in recent months from internationalisation of the renminbi project with banks' net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases and the trade data undergoing large swings, and policymakers would be reluctant to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  become more volatile.\par \par \uc2 Today's gains open up the renminbi to take a swipe at a couple of chart targets which, if broken, would signal further bullishness for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trend.\par \par \uc2 First up is the 6.1541 per dollar level, the 50 percent retracement of the entire 2014 trading range followed by the 6.15 per dollar line, the 200-day moving average.\par \par \uc2 CONNECTING MARKETS\par \par \uc2 Growing optimism from global investors towards Chinese equities has also come at a time when policymakers are working around the clock to implement an initiative to connect the Shanghai and the Hong Kong stock markets, fuelling inflows.\par \par \uc2 Capital inflows in anticipation of the scheme has driven up the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar forcing the city's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, while the offshore yuan has started to trade at a premium to its mainland counterpart.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's recent rally, supported by signs of recovery in exports, has wiped out half of the 3.3 percent of losses incurred at the depth of its weakness in late April. That was when the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  punished speculators by engineering a dramatic and sustained decline in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 With trade-related flows starting to be supportive, banks are becoming more bullish on the yuan. \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , for one, expects the yuan to rise to 6.14 per dollar by end-2014. (Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-7T04:18:26.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140807ea87001bc\par }\page {