\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to defend  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2 's dollar peg, sells HK$1.16 bln\b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2014\par 11:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Aug 5 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and sold HK$1.16 billion ($149.7 million) in Hong Kong dollars on Tuesday as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the strong end of its trading range.               \par \par \uc2 According to the HKMA, the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lift the aggregate balance - the sum of balances on clearing accounts maintained by banks with the HKMA - to HK$239.189 billion on Aug. 7.               \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar, but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85. Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, the HKMA is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hits 7.75 or 7.85 to keep the band intact.               \par \par \uc2 Heavy capital inflows into Hong Kong coincide with some Russian companies, including mobile phone operator Megafon  , saying they have converted their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits into Hong Kong dollars to protect against any further Western sanctions.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-5T10:33:01.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140805ea850069e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
