\par\par \b \uc2 Risks Remain in China Banks Despite Securitization: \uc2 Moody's\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2014\par 05:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] As Chinese regulators start to allow the country's banks to package loans as marketable securities, risks remain in China's banking system, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says in a report issued Monday. Chinese banks are securitizing assets to remove the associated risks from their balance sheets, but they haven't managed to entirely diversify the risks from the banking sector. That's because "a large majority of investors" in securitization transactions in China are also the banks themselves, the ratings agency says. "Risk is simply shifted from one bank to the other," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. In order to really diversify the risks away from the banking system, it says, "a diversification and deepening of the investor base are required." (Lingling.Wei@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 27 Jul 2014 23:56 ET  Risks Remain in China Banks Despite Securitization: \uc2 Moody's\uc2  -- Market Talk\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0356 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] As Chinese regulators start to allow the country's banks to package loans as marketable securities, risks remain in China's banking system, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  says in a report issued Monday. Chinese banks are securitizing assets to remove the associated risks from their balance sheets, but they haven't managed to entirely diversify the risks from the banking sector. That's because "a large majority of investors" in securitization transactions in China are also the banks themselves, the ratings agency says. "Risk is simply shifted from one bank to the other," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. In order to really diversify the risks away from the banking system, it says, "a diversification and deepening of the investor base are required." (Lingling.Wei@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0353 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Even though earnings momentum in Taiwan is reaching levels that haven't typically been sustained for long, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks there is still juice left in the market. "Yes, momentum is at high levels in Tech, but top-down and bottom-up we think this probably persists a bit longer, and the good news at the market level is that it might be extending to other important sectors like Financials," it says. Taiwan also stands in "stark contrast" to the lackluster rest of region, it says. "This continues to make Taiwan look more positive in relative terms in the absence of any strong earnings story elsewhere in Asia ex Japan at the country level." (jacky.wong@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jackycwong)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0349 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Hong Kong dollar lingers near 7.75 a dollar--the strong side of the band it can trade in--amid abundant cash inflows, triggering again the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the market last Friday. "Capital influx is reflected in the recent stocks rally," said a senior trader at a local bank. Hang Seng Index is up 0.9%, hitting the highest since Nov 2010. The HKMA injected HK$5.35 billion into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market last Friday, boosting the total injection this month to HK$49 billion, as it's obliged to step in once the HKD hits either side of the 7.75-7.85 trading band vs the dollar. The HKMA deputy chief \f28 \uc2 \f2 executive Peter Pang\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a column he expects the local unit to stay strong in the near term amid a slew of stock listings, M&A activities and dividend distributions in the market. (fiona.law@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0346 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's trade data in June expected at 6:00am GMT Monday will determine if the country's ruling military will be under pressure to find local sources of growth to restore the economy. Thai exports have been weak this year in spite of gradual recovery in the global economy. The ministry's permanent secretary revealed last Friday the preliminary export figure of 7.2% growth, the first increase on-year since February. Several economists predict an improvement--that exports will swing to growth and the contraction in imports will ease--but say that the trade balance will remain in deficit. Hong Kong-based HSBC Global Research predicts that the trade balance deficit will widen to almost one billion dollars from $809 million in May. (warangkana.chomchuen@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0316 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The window for private equity firms to offload assets on the Australian market, trading near six-year highs, is still open, judging by the early gains in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Healthscope\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HSO.AU) as it rejoins the ASX. The IPO, which was priced at the upper end of the range indicated ahead of the offer, joins a tide of private equity listings that include this year's float of in vitro fertilization firms \f28 \uc2 \f2 Monash IVF\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MVF.AU) last month and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Virtus Health\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VRT.AU) a year earlier, both of which rose on their ASX debut. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Healthscope\f28 \uc2 \f2  was taken private in 2010 in a buyout by a consortium of funds managed by TPG and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carlyle Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which will continue to hold a 38% stake. Its shares are trading at A$2.13, up 1.4% on the offer price. (robb.stewart@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0302 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A rally driven by a reported 1 trillion yuan stimulus from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  raises more questions than answers, says Bank of Communications' global equity strategist Hao Hong. Chinese equity markets rallied last week after reports that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  had channeled 1 trillion yuan to \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  using its "pledged supplementary lending" facility. But it looks more like "accounting sleight of hand", BoCOM says. "The PBoC's balance sheet has not expanded by this amount, despite that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 CDB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's balance had an increase of >1 trillion interbank deposits - in April." The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has said nothing on the matter. "The reason for such a covert maneuver is anybody's guess," Hao Hong says. China's slowdown, bad as it is, is not yet severe enough to justify a CNY1 trillion stimulus anyway, he adds. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @gregorhunter)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0252 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The yuan rises--extending a trend started early last week as investor interest in the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  picked up after recent good economic data. USD/CNY is at 6.1872 after hitting 6.1863--another more-than-three-month high for the yuan compared with 6.1915 Friday close. But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to guide the yuan weaker via a daily reference rate to track the dollar's strength overnight. The central parity is set at 6.1622 compared with 6.1597 Friday. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems want to take the chance to push the spot rate and the fixing closer which may facilitate its guidance in the market," a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader says. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.2413/6.2456 from 6.2380/6.2430 late Friday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0231 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China shares are higher, extending gains for a fifth session as more funds flow into blue chips like banks with low valuations. Interest in a direct stock-investment program between Shanghai and Hong Kong is also heating up, analysts says. The Shanghai Composite Index gains 1.3% to 2153.80, and is tipped to face resistance at 2200. "The market is extending its recent upward trend thanks to continuous strength in blue chips," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Soochow Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Deng Wenyuan. The index might head further north if trading volume expands. BoCom (601328.SH) jumps 6.0% to CNY4.08 while ICBC (601398.SH) rises 1.7% to CNY3.58. The Shenzhen Index adds 0.6% to 1118.60. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0223 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 GCL-Poly Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (3800.HK) shares jump Monday after the solar-panel parts supplier said it expects to post its second-straight half in the black. Expectations are running high for a profitable year for \f28 \uc2 \f2 GCL-Poly\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which hasn't posted a year in the black since 2011, according to FactSet. Nomura, though, warns the Street is getting ahead of itself as solar-parts demand remains relatively weak and inventories high. It reiterates a reduce rating on the stock, which it says is "expensive." Shares rise 3.9% to HK$2.69. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0213 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan shares fall 0.3% to 9407.47 as tech heavyweights continue to retreat from record highs. TSMC (2330.TW) is down 0.8% at NT$122.00, Hon Hai (2317.TW) drops 0.9% to NT$107.00; while TPK (3673.TW) falls 4.3% to NT$212.50, on top of a 6.9% decline on Friday, after the touch-panel maker gives a cautious forecast for 3Q amid rising competition. First Capital says the Taiex is biased downward now, with profit-taking to continue to weigh on tech counters. It expects immediate support at 9335. (aries.poon@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "USD/JPY Has Room to Touch 101.20 for Now -- Market Talk," at 0118 GMT, misstated the USD/JPY in the headline and the text. The USD/JPY "has room for upward swing" to touch around 102.20, not 101.20.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0203 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Analysts are watching changes in net-gearing levels and profit margins of listed Chinese property developers for a clearer picture of weaknesses and pressures they face amid the current housing downturn. Listed developers start reporting first-half earnings from July 31. "For liquidity, we also look at cash on hand and short-term debt ratio, which is a good indicator of developers' short term (1-year) financial risk, says CIMB Securities analyst Johnson Hu. "We continue to prefer China developers over Hong Kong property companies due to cheaper valuations and also very low expectations," says Barclays Research head of property sector, Paul Louie, whose top picks are China Overseas Land & Investment(0688.HK), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shimao Property Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0813.HK) and KWG (1813.HK) (Esther.fung@wsj.com, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @estherfung)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 27, 2014 23:56 ET (03:56 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140728ea7s0008f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
