\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong must stay alert to capital outflows despite  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 's strength -HKMA\b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2014\par 05:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, July 27 (Reuters) - Hong Kong must stay alert to the possibility of large capital outflows even as its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had unusual strength recently due to inflows for acquisitions, share-dividends and related transactions, a senior official at its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Since July 1, the local dollar has repeatedly hit the strong end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar. That caused the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to absorb more than $5.6 billion in inflows and inject more than 40 billion Hong Kong dollars into local money markets.\par \par \uc2 While the inflows will likely boost prices and increase liquidity, \uc2 Peter Pang\uc2 , the HKMA's deputy chief executive warned that the turmoil seen in emerging markets last year due to large-scale outflows showed that fund flows could change directions quickly.\par \par \uc2 "As the U.S. economy recovers and its monetary environment normalises, there remain considerable uncertainties in the future direction of fund flows," Pang said in an article posted on HKMA's website Saturday night. (For a link: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/1otXVtg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/1otXVtg}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Pang cited four reasons for the local dollar's recent strength: large dividend payments by local companies, a rise in cross-border deal activity, precautionary demand by banks due to the half-yearly close and likely investor positioning ahead of the launch of a cross-border stock exchange programme.\par \par \uc2 Citic Pacific\uc2 's acquisition of its parent's assets and the purchase of local lender \uc2 Wing Hang Bank\uc2  by Singapore's OCBC totalled nearly 100 billion Hong Kong dollars ($12.90 billion) while dividend distributions by locally-listed companies are set to hit 200 billion Hong Kong dollars, according to the HKMA.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar, but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85. Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, the HKMA is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hits either end of the band.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong introduced the peg in 1983, amid evaporating confidence as China and the UK negotiated the future of what was then a British colony. The peg has helped Hong Kong become a major financial centre and trade hub.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 7.7499 Hong Kong Dollars) (Reporting by Saikat Chatterjee; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-7-27T04:15:37.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140727ea7r00209\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
