\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Lower on Wall Street Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 1,099 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2014\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par               Latest  Change %Change \par DJIA         16276.69 -26.08 -0.16% \par Nasdaq        4226.39 -50.40 -1.18% \par S&P 500       1857.44  -9.08 -0.49% \par FTSE 100      6520.39 -36.78 -0.56% \par Nikkei Stock 14410.34 -64.96 -0.45% \par Hang Seng    21771.38 -75.07 -0.34% \par Kospi         1942.26  -3.29 -0.17% \par SGX Nifty     6596.50 -12.00 -0.18% \par March 2014 contract \par  \par           Latest   Change \par USD/JPY 102.21-22  -0.03% \par Range   102.27-102.16 \par EUR/USD 1.3831-34  -0.05% \par Range   1.3839-1.3832 \par  \par CBOT Wheat May 2014  $7.144 per bushel (Settlement Price) \par                   Last     Change \par Comex Gold       $1,311.20  -$24.80 \par Comex Gold Range $1,308.50-$1,335.70 \par Nymex Crude (NY)   $99.38    -$0.08 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks declined as a selloff in the biotechnology sector persisted for a second straight session while investors also focused on negative economic data out of China and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Sellers continued to single out the once hot sector, which was among the top performers in last year's banner rally, as investors and analysts say concerns have mounted that many companies in the sector had become too expensive.\par \par \uc2 The catalyst behind the recent slide emerged Friday with a congressional complaint over the high price of a \uc2 Gilead Sciences\uc2 ' hepatitis C drug. Although the complaint singled out one company, investors and analysts say the decline across the sector reflects broader concerns about valuations across the sector. "Sentiment is waning the most we've seen in the last [two to three] years," analysts at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  wrote.\par \par \uc2 "The success that some of the stocks have enjoyed is making them more vulnerable," said Michael Farr, president of the portfolio-management firm Farr, Miller Washington, which manages about $950 million and owns biotechnology stocks. "This strikes me still as a short-term blip, but that doesn't say it couldn't be more permanent."\par \par \uc2 Shares of Gilead bounced back from their early Monday losses, but shares of other biotech companies, including \uc2 Biogen Idec\uc2 , Celgene and \uc2 Alexion Pharmaceuticals\uc2 , declined.\par \par \uc2 "The momentum guys are just coming out of these stocks," said Tom Carter, managing director at brokerage JonesTrading. He added that negative economic data globally was also denting sentiment. "It just gives people pause."\par \par \uc2 Some corners of the market were stronger. Shares of Apple gained 2% after The Wall Street Journal reported the technology company was in talks with \uc2 Comcast\uc2  for a streaming-TV service that would use Apple set-top boxes. In addition, Apple's content would get special treatment on \uc2 Comcast\uc2 's cables to bypass congestion on the Web, the report said.\par \par \uc2 Among other stock movers, Herbalife gained 6.7% after the supplement producer agreed to nominate three more designees of activist investor Carl Icahn to its board of directors. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks in Tokyo were dented Tuesday by a firmer yen while Chinese shares in Hong Kong fell as skepticism emerged around hoped-for stimulus measures. Japan's Nikkei 225 fell 0.5% to 14403.09.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Kospi slipped 0.1% to 1942.69 while Australia's S&P/ASX 200 fell 0.5% to 5318.40.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A downbeat session on Wall Street also weighed on sentiment in Asia. U.S. stocks slumped overnight amid a selloff in biotechnology shares, hitting the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite the hardest. The Nasdaq fell 1.2% to 4226.39 while the S&P 500 declined 0.5% to 1857.44.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Closer to home, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  data released Tuesday showed Prime Minster Shinzo Abe is having trouble getting Japanese corporations to put their large cash piles to work in the economy. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and deposits held by private-sector companies excluding financial firms rose 6.4% year-over-year to Yen222 trillion ($2.17 trillion) as of the end of December, following a 5.8% rise in the previous three month period, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rising expectations that China will introduce new measures to tackle slowing growth was met with skepticism in some corners Tuesday. Hong Kong's Hang Seng China Enterprises Index rallied 2.8% Monday despite worse-than-expected Chinese manufacturing data. Stocks tied to the country's so-called "old economy"--such as oil and bank stocks--were the strongest gainers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists at Reorient Financial Markets in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  warned investors Tuesday against expecting an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Chinese government. "China's new leaders have proved on several occasions that they will not be nervously diverted from their course of action by minor setbacks," the economists wrote. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthened slightly versus the dollar, trading around Y102.23 versus Y102.25 late Monday in New York. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper prices were little changed at the close of trade at the London Metal Exchange Monday after initial concerns over some weaker-than-expected Chinese manufacturing data were tempered by hopes for further economic stimulus in that country. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. oil futures rose Monday as traders focused on the closure of the Houston Ship Channel and the possibility of more economic sanctions by the West against Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude for May delivery settled up 14 cents, or 0.1%, at $99.60 a barrel on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 New York Mercantile Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . U.S. oil futures have risen four of the past five sessions. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par Australian Prime Minister: Search to Resume as Soon as Possible \par China's Harbin Bank Raises $1.1 Bln in HK IPO; Prices Close to Low End of Price Range - Sources \par Australia Suspends Search for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia Airlines\f28 \uc2 \f2  Flight 370 \par G-7 Agrees to Exclude Russia, Boost Sanctions \par BOJ: Japan Firms' Cash Holdings Up 6.4% on Year at End-Dec \par SEC Is Probing Dealings by Banks and Companies in Loan Securities \par Huawei's Role in Internet Traffic Grows \par Apple, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Comcast\f28 \uc2 \f2  Likely to Invite Scrutiny \par GM, Ford Struggle to Crack India's Car Market \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  Catches Lift From Technology Overhaul \par Box Inc Publicly Files IPO \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2  Promotes ABC News President to Oversee TV Group \par South Korea to Spend $6.8B on Lockheed Jets \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  Deal Delayed By Regulators \par Mark Mobius: Events in Ukraine Will Continue to Weigh on Markets \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Merge Voice, Electronic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Trading \par RBS, Sumitomo Mitsui Hold Preliminary Talks About RBS Citizens Deal: Sources \par NY Financial Regulator Says Fix Under Review By Other States for 'Shadow Insurance' Is Flawed -- Let