\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Mostly Higher; Australia Hits 5-Year High -- 2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2013\par 06:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Regional stocks make moderate gains\par \par \uc2 --Australian dollar falls after jobs report\par \par \uc2 --Apple suppliers remain under pressure\par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Daniel Inman \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian markets were mostly higher on Thursday, with Sydney hitting a fresh five-year intraday high, though a stronger yen weighed on Tokyo shares.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With a positive lead from the U.S., Asia was able to move higher despite the absence of major trading cues. Stocks were moderating after strong gains earlier in the week brought about by further signs of a Chinese economic recovery and reduced expectations of military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Syria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese companies listed in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  managed to add to their gains, with the Hang Seng \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index up 0.4%. The index is already up 8.7% month-to-date. Hong Kong's broader Hang Seng Index was up 0.2%, and the Shanghai Composite rose 0.1% on the mainland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After rising 0.6% Wednesday to close at a multi-year high, the S&P/ASX 200 pushed higher to hit the highest intraday to more than a five-year high of 5252.00. The market was last up 0.3% at 5248.50.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar, however, which hit a 10-week high overnight against its U.S. counterpart, tumbled after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australian Bureau of Statistics\f28 \uc2 \f2  said that the number of people employed fell 10,800 in August compared with an expected 10,000 rise. The Aussie recently changed hands at US$0.9256 compared with US$0.9343 earlier in the session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen strengthened overnight--below Y100 to the dollar mark--and continued to firm in Asia. It was last at Y99.53 to the dollar compared with Y99.89 late Wednesday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The stronger Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weighed on stocks, with the Nikkei down 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2  sank 6.8% in Tokyo after a Nikkei report said the car company is planning a Y200 billion public offering this fiscal year, in order to buy back preferred shares owned by four \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  group companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Electronics firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sharp Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . was also under pressure, losing 6% in Tokyo after news reports said the company plans to raise up to Y200 billion through an offering of shares in order to help cover a shortfall in its corporate pension plan in the current fiscal year and pay for bond redemptions in 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regional suppliers to Apple remained under pressure after Apple sank 5.4% overnight, following disappointment surrounding the firm's latest iPhones. Foster Electric Company lost 3.6% in Japan and \f28 \uc2 \f2 AAC Technologies Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 3.3% in Hong Kong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere in the region, South Korea's Kospi was up 0.4%, and Singapore's Straits Times Index rose 0.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More generally, markets were looking ahead to next week, when the U.S. Federal Reserve will hold its policy meeting, which some expect will result in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reducing the scale of its bond-buying program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Daniel Inman at daniel.inman@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this o