\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE: Asian Shares Mixed; HK, Australia Up On \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Comments\b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2013\par 11:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Australia gain, \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -- Chinese banks recover strongly in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -- Nikkei pares gains as yen strengthens.\par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Daniel Inman \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Signs that China's credit crunch is starting to ease pushed stocks in Hong Kong and Australia higher, but elsewhere worries lingered over the health of the banking system even after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moved to reassure investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets in the region have been increasingly anxious over a liquidity squeeze that some feared could start to threaten growth in Asia's biggest economy. While some of the worries dissipated Wednesday as liquidity conditions eased slightly after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday it would try to bring to an end the squeeze, it wasn't enough to aid Shanghai where the benchmark index fell 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Signs of continued stress showed up in the bond market too, where China had to pay higher borrowing costs at a $2.1 billion government bond sale in Hong Kong's offshore yuan market. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold three-year bonds at a coupon of 2.87%, far higher than the 1.85% it paid at its last sale of similar bonds in 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Local banks have been facing problems of tight yuan liquidity recently, because of the cash crunch in mainland China in recent weeks," said Becky Liu, a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Chartered\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst. "Therefore they can't support the auction as much this time round."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Executives also stressed their worries with Mark Cutifani, Chief Executive of giant miner \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anglo American\f28 \uc2 \f2 , saying the fallout from the cash crunch could mean less investment in China which would hurt commodities. Copper and Nickel have both fallen to their lowest level in years this week, while gold fell as much as 2.6% Wednesday and silver dropped 4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But there were reasons for some optimism, with China's seven-day repurchase agreement rate--a benchmark of borrowing costs between banks- falling to 7.30%, down from 7.44% at Tuesday's close. That followed the central's bank statement Tuesday saying it had introduced funds to some financial institutions in recent days, providing relief for cash-strapped banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese stocks listed in Hong Kong recovered, with the Hang Seng \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  index jumping 3.3% to 9164.64 - its second gain in the last 20 sessions. The index was helped by banks that have fallen heavily in recent weeks. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Industrial and Commercial Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 6.8% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  advanced 6.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Minsheng Banking Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., one of the banks that was sold off particularly hard, rallied 6.3% in Hong Kong, but its Shanghai-listing was down 1.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia, a country with strong trade ties to China also made a gain, with the S&P/ASX 200 up 1.6% to 4731.70 - breaking a four-session losing streak.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, Japan's Nikkei was down 1% at 12834.01 as local stocks were initially supported by a weaker yen, but moved downwards after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pushed back against the dollar and Shanghai failed to make a recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rose to Y98.20, compared with Y97.82 late Tuesday in New York, but was last at Y97.49.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There was also a strong recovery in Southeast Asia, a region that has been hit particularly badly in the recent weeks due to emerging-market outflows. The Philippines PSE Composite jumped 5.7% to 6118.94, recovering some of the 11.2% loss that it made over the previous five sessions, and continuing the volatile trading that has seen the market make large moves up or down on a daily basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Kospi was up 0.2% at 1783.45.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Daniel Inman at daniel.inman@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 26-06-13 0950GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130626e96q000cw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
