\par\par \b \uc2 China shares slip, pull Hong Kong down from 16-month high\b0\par\par\uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2012\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * HSI -0.3, H-shares -0.2 pct\par \par \uc2 * CSI300 -1.1 pct, Shanghai Comp -1.0 pct\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 CLP Holdings\uc2  down after equity offering\par \par \uc2 * Foreign investors remain optimistic on China\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 HSBC\uc2  sees Shang Comp at 2,500 at end-2013\par \par \uc2 (updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Vikram Subhedar\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Dec 13 (Reuters) - Hong Kong shares retreated from a 16-month high on Thursday as concern over the U.S. "fiscal cliff" and a weak day on mainland markets triggered profit-taking after the recent rally.\par \par \uc2 The Hang Seng Index eased 0.3 percent to 22,445.6. The \uc2 China Enterprises\uc2  index of top locally listed mainland firms fell 0.2 percent but extended its outperformance over China's domestic indexes.\par \par \uc2 On the mainland, the CSI300 fell 1.1 percent while the Shanghai Composite closed down 1.0 percent with large-cap energy and banking shares the biggest drags.\par \par \uc2 Utilities were weak in Hong Kong, led by a 3.6 percent drop for \uc2 CLP Holdings\uc2  after it raised $982 million in new shares to fund expansion. Power Assets fell 1.4 percent while \uc2 China Resources Power\uc2  dropped 4.2 percent and was the day's worst performing Hang Seng component.\par \par \uc2 Corporates and investors have taken advantage of the recent rally in Hong Kong to sell shares in the open market.\par \par \uc2 Shares of property firm Kaisa Holdings fell 5.8 percent after private equity firm \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2  said it was selling up to $67 million of stock in the company.\par \par \uc2 A combination of global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing and steadily improving Chinese economic data has spurred revived interest among foreign investors, causing money to flow back into \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again on Wednesday to defend the Hong Kong dollar's peg which has come under pressure because of the inflows.\par \par \uc2 WORRY OVER CLIFF DAMAGE\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  pledged to keep interest rates low till the unemployment rate drops to 6.5 percent and extended the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's asset purchase programme, but reiterated that monetary policy won't be enough to offset damage from going over the "fiscal cliff".\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, investors are still fretting about the U.S. political stand-off.\par \par \uc2 "If there's some bad news about the fiscal cliff, then yes, you will probably see a pull back," said David Gaud, a senior portfolio manager at Edmond de Rothschild Asset Management in Hong Kong, referring to the Hang Seng.\par \par \uc2 But he added that China is looking increasingly attractive.\par \par \uc2 "The earnings momentum is probably strengthening and for the first time in nearly two years in China we may start to get some positive surprises," said Gaud.\par \par \uc2 On the mainland, investors locked in some profits after China's indexes hit one-month highs earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 ICBC fell 1.3 percent and was the biggest drag on the CSI300 followed by a 0.6 percent slide for Petrochina .\par \par \uc2 Kweichow Moutai shares, which rebounded earlier this week, following a contamination scare eased 2.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 China's domestic markets are poised for their third straight annual loss this year although there is increasing bullishness about the market's prospects next year.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  maintained its "overweight" view on China but cut its rating on Hong Kong to "underweight" from "neutral", citing valuations.\par \par \uc2 "The most obvious value market in Asia is China," said Herald van der Linde, head of equity strategy, Asia-Pacific at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . At 9.7 times forward price-to-earnings, China is Asia's second-cheapest market after Korea, according to the broker.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is forecasting that the Shanghai Composite will be at 2,500 at the end of 2013, a rise of 21 percent from where it ended on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-HONGKONG-CHINA-STOCKS/UPDATE 2 | ABX | ABN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | D | E | RBN | M | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121213e8cd000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
