\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong, China shares weaker as rally pauses; \uc2 Foxconn\uc2  surges\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2012\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * HSI -0.5 pct, CSI300 -0.2 pct, Shanghai Comp -0.1 pct\par \par \uc2 * Turnover dips a fourth as caution sets in ahead of US elections\par \par \uc2 * Financials weak on profit-taking, fund inflow hopes aid developers\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Foxconn\uc2  posts biggest ever single-day jump after Citi upgrade\par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Vikram Subhedar\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Nov 5 (Reuters) - Hong Kong shares eased slightly on Monday, as caution ahead of the U.S. presidential elections spurred mild profit-taking in financials after a strong run-up last week lifted the Hang Seng Index to a 15-month high.\par \par \uc2 The Hang Seng ended 0.5 percent down at 22,006.4 points, after recording a 2.6 percent gain last week. The index is up nearly 20 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Bucking the overall weak trend were shares of \uc2 Foxconn International Holdings\uc2 , the world's biggest contract maker of cellphones, which surged as much as 35 percent after \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  upgraded the stock to a 'buy' and said it expected the firm to start assembling iPhones this year. [I D :nL3E8M50KF]\par \par \uc2 Shares closed up 31.9 percent in their biggest ever single-day jump adding $820 million to \uc2 Foxconn International\uc2 's market value. \uc2 Foxconn\uc2  shares are still down nearly 28 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 The China Enterprises Index, one of Asia's best performing benchmarks last month, was down 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 On the mainland, the CSI300 of the top Shanghai and Shenzhen listings closed down 0.2 percent and the Shanghai Composite eased 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Essentially risk appetite is still there ,but it's a bit subdued," said Tom Kaan, a director at Louis Capital Markets in Hong Kong, adding that the market may be taking its lead from stocks on Wall Street which fell despite better-than-expected jobs data.\par \par \uc2 "But the flow of funds into Asia is on and Hong Kong is one of the biggest beneficiaries," said Kaan, recommending investors to keep adding blue chips such as \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  on any weakness.\par \par \uc2 Persistent capital inflows into \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forced the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  10 times in two weeks to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg as money flowing in pushed the Hong Kong dollar to the strong end of its trading range.\par \par \uc2 Hopes of continued inflows supported Hong Kong property shares, the only sub-sector to trade higher on Monday. The property sub-index was up 0.3 percent .\par \par \uc2 Those gains were not enough, however, to offset profit-taking in index heavyweights such as \uc2 HSBC Holdings\par \par \uc2 and Industrial and Commercial Bank of China (ICBC)\par \par \uc2 which fell 0.3 percent and 1.3 percent, respectively, after their rally last month.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  reported third-quarter results after market hours and said it posted profits before taxes of $3.5 billion and said potential financial penalties in the U.S. for lax anti-money laundering controls in Mexico could be significantly higher than the amount it had set aside.\par \par \uc2 The bank took a $1.15 billion hit to cover a potential fine in the U.S and UK mis-selling, having earlier set aside $700 million in July.\par \par \uc2 Its London-listed shares were trading down 2.5 percent, extending losses after the earnings.\par \par \uc2 Financials were weak on the mainland as well with China Life\par \par \uc2 down 0.8 percent and rival insurer Ping An\par \par \uc2 off 0.7 percent. Banking shares were on the backfoot led b